export const PRODUCT = {

catalog: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Product Catalog",
  icon: "\u{1F4E6}",
  color: "#10b981",
  description: "Product Catalog is the foundation for defining and organizing the products and services offered through Revenue Cloud. It models simple and bundled products, product variants, Dynamic Attributes, Product Classifications, catalogs, categories, qualification rules, Product Selling Models, and product relationships. Product Discovery uses these definitions for structured browsing, search, filtering, recommendations, and Guided Product Selection. Catalog products are assigned to price books for pricing and provide the shared product definitions used by Product Configurator, while access is managed through permission-set assignments and sharing settings.",
  components: [
    {id:"product-management",name:"Product Management",icon:"\u{1F4CB}",desc:"Product Management defines the items and services that a business offers through Revenue Cloud. Product Catalog Management supports simple products, bundled products, configurable products, static products, product variants, qualification rules, and Product Selling Models. Product classifications and Dynamic Attributes provide reusable structures for creating products with shared characteristics. These product definitions are also used by Product Configurator and can be assigned to price books for pricing.",tags:["Product2","ProductAttribute","ProductAttributeSet"],docs:["Products represent the items and services sold to customers and can also be nonsellable. A simple product has no associated product hierarchy, while a bundled product groups products that are sold together as one unit. The Product Type and Configure During Sale fields determine whether a product is simple or bundled and whether it is static or configurable. Static products can't have child products, quantities, or attributes changed at run time, while configurable products support these changes during product configuration.","Bundled products use a hierarchy that begins with a root product and can include product component groups, product components, and product classification components. Configurable bundles require groups, and their child components can include static or configurable simple and bundled products. Local cardinality controls whether components are required or selected by default and defines permitted quantities. Group cardinality controls how many distinct child components users can select at run time.","Product variants group purchasable products that share a common identity but differ in characteristics such as size, color, or storage capacity. A variation parent organizes the related variations but isn't itself purchasable, while each variation product has its own stock keeping unit. Product Selling Models optionally define whether a product is sold one time, for a defined term, or as an evergreen offering. Qualification rules can further determine product eligibility and availability during browsing based on criteria stored in Salesforce objects."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.product_catalog_introduction.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Products are assigned to price books for pricing"},{"planet":"configurator","desc":"Products use configuration rules for bundling"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Products appear as line items on quotes and orders"}]},
    {id:"categories-catalogs",name:"Categories and Catalogs",icon:"\u{1F5C2}",desc:"Categories and Catalogs organize Revenue Cloud product offerings for structured browsing. A catalog is a collection of products arranged into categories, and it must exist before categories are created or products are assigned. Categories and subcategories provide a hierarchical structure, and a product can be assigned to more than one category or subcategory. Category sort order controls the sequence presented during product browsing.",tags:["ProductCatalog","ProductCategory","ProductCategoryProduct"],docs:["Catalogs are the highest-level containers used to organize products for sale. A catalog can represent a type of offering, a sales territory, or a business unit, and at least one catalog is required. Catalog records can include descriptive information, effective dates, a unique catalog code, and a type such as Sales or Service Process. Products become discoverable through the catalog after they are assigned to its categories.","Categories group similar products and services within a catalog and can contain subcategories for more granular organization. The Categories tab presents the hierarchy and supports adding, reordering, expanding, and collapsing subcategories. Category records can specify a parent category, a category code, and whether the category is exposed as a navigational breadcrumb through APIs. Products must be created before they can be assigned to categories or subcategories.","Sort Order determines how categories appear during the purchase flow and in Product Discovery. Categories with a null sort order appear before negative, zero, and positive values, with ties resolved by creation timestamp in descending order. Catalog administrators can also set a default catalog in Product Discovery Settings. For more complex transactions, the product list page or flow can allow users to move between catalogs."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Catalogs determine which products are available during quoting"}]},
    {id:"attributes-classifications",name:"Attributes and Classifications",icon:"\u{1F3F7}",desc:"Attributes and Classifications provide reusable, data-driven definitions for product characteristics in Revenue Cloud. Dynamic Attributes can represent text, number, date, datetime, checkbox, picklist, currency, and percent values. Product classifications act as templates that assign attributes to products and support inherited attribute hierarchies. Product-level and bundle-context overrides tailor inherited values without changing the underlying reusable definition.",tags:["ProductAttribute","ProductAttributeSet","ProductClassification"],docs:["Dynamic Attributes describe product characteristics such as color, memory, storage, display size, or warranty period. Attribute definitions specify the data type, label, default value, description, help text, and whether the attribute is active or required. Picklist attributes use reusable Attribute Picklist and Attribute Picklist Value records, and active picklist values can be included or excluded for particular classifications or products. Attribute categories can organize related attributes into logical groups for easier reuse.","Product classifications are reusable templates for products that share similar characteristics. Attributes are assigned to a product classification individually or through an attribute category, and products associated with the classification inherit those attributes automatically. Subclassifications can inherit attributes from a parent classification while adding or overriding attributes for more specialized products. This structure reduces repetitive setup and helps maintain consistent product definitions.","Inherited attributes can be configured at the product level to change settings such as default value, visibility, editability, requirement status, sequence, or run-time display type. Picklist values inherited from a classification can also be included or excluded for an individual product. Within a bundle, Product Component Attributes Override localizes changes to a component without modifying the product used elsewhere. If an attribute definition changes, administrators must review corresponding classification, product, and bundle override values for consistency."],connections:[{"planet":"configurator","desc":"Attribute values drive configuration rule evaluation"}]},
    {id:"product-discovery",name:"Product Discovery",icon:"\u{1F50D}",desc:"Product Discovery provides structured product browsing, search, filtering, and selection for Revenue Cloud users. It presents product images, descriptions, prices, bundle details, and attributes through Product List, Product Details, Product Bundle Details, and Product Attribute Details components. Search options include SOQL based search, indexed search, and Product Field Search, with faceted search available for indexed catalog data. Guided Product Selection uses questions and responses to identify products that match customer requirements.",tags:["ProductSearch","GuidedSelling"],docs:["Product Discovery uses ProductDiscoveryContext or an extended context definition to populate browsing data. Administrators can optionally configure a qualification procedure and a pricing procedure that use the same context definition. Qualification rules determine whether products and categories are available, while the Product Discovery Pricing Procedure supplies list prices. Product Catalog Management Designer users can customize the browsing experience through flows, Lightning App Builder, and Experience Builder.","Indexed search supports configurable term-based searches across product fields and Dynamic Attributes, faceted search, and Guided Product Selection. Searchable and filterable fields are configured in Index and Search Configuration, and product catalog changes require an appropriate full or partial index rebuild. Product Field Search searches text-indexed Product2 fields such as product name, description, product code, and stock keeping unit for very large catalogs. Semantic Search can interpret synonyms, spelling variations, and product concepts across indexed product data.","Guided Product Selection is accessed through the Guide Me button and uses assessment questions in an Omniscript form. User responses are stored through the ProductGuidedSelectionIntegration Omniscript and used to filter the product list. Product Discovery can also show rule-based product recommendations that honor qualification rules. With enhanced Product Discovery features enabled, transaction preview, configuration rule outcomes, recommendations, and Auto Save keep catalog browsing synchronized with the current transaction."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Discovered products are added to quotes and orders"}]},
    {id:"product-relationships",name:"Product Relationships",icon:"\u{1F517}",desc:"Product Relationships connect products, classifications, and component groups within Revenue Cloud product structures. A relationship can represent the parent-child connections that form a product bundle and can span multiple levels. Product Related Component records define component quantities, optionality, quantity editing, pricing inclusion, and other run-time behavior. Product Component Group records organize related components and apply group-level cardinality and display sequencing.",tags:["ProductRelationship","ProductRelationshipType","ProductBundle"],docs:["A bundled product starts with a root product and contains child components connected through product relationships. Product components use Bundle to Bundle Component Relationship, while product classification components use Bundle to Product Classification Component Relationship. Adding a product classification component makes products based on that classification available dynamically at run time. Product component groups provide logical organization and are mandatory for configurable bundles but optional for static bundles.","Local cardinality determines whether a component is required, included by default, or editable in quantity, and it defines default, minimum, and maximum quantities. Group cardinality determines the minimum and maximum number of distinct child components users can add from a group. Quantity Scaling Method controls whether child quantity is independent, constant, or proportional to parent quantity. Product Component Attributes Override and component or group cardinality overrides remain scoped to the root bundle in which they are defined.","The Structure tab is used to add, inspect, and maintain groups, products, product classifications, and their relationships. Deleting a group also deletes the components under that group from the bundle structure. Product definition validation checks bundle cardinality and structural requirements and reports corrective actions. Catalog products and their relationships provide the shared product hierarchy consumed by Product Configurator at run time."],connections:[{"planet":"configurator","desc":"Relationships define bundle structures and product rules"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Relationships influence product suggestions during quoting"}]},
    {id:"import-export",name:"Import and Export",icon:"\u{1F4E5}",desc:"Import and Export supports controlled loading, translation, migration, and validation of Revenue Cloud product catalog data. Advanced CSV Data Import uses CSV templates and Data Processing Engine definitions to create Product Catalog Management records. Translation data can be exported, localized, and imported for supported products, categories, classification attributes, and picklist values. Migration tools can also extract and move catalog structures such as products, bundles, attributes, and price books between implementations.",tags:["ProductImport","ProductExport","DataImportService"],docs:["Catalog administrators and product designers can download predefined CSV templates from the Product Catalog Management home page and populate them with catalog data. Data Processing Engine definition templates map CSV column headers to Product Catalog Management object fields, and administrators clone and activate these definitions before import. Custom fields require corresponding CSV columns and mappings in the Data Source and Writeback Object nodes. The CSV File Import page starts the import and provides a generated log when processing completes.","Product catalog translations can be exported in STF or XLIFF format, edited with localized values, and imported back into Salesforce. Supported translated data includes Product2 names and descriptions, category names and descriptions, product classification attribute labels and picklist values, and Dynamic Attribute picklist display values. Imported translations become available to Product Discovery after the required index rebuild. Administrators can also enter smaller sets of translations manually from a record's Translations tab.","For migrations from an existing Salesforce CPQ implementation, the product catalog is migrated before transactional data because downstream records depend on it. Migration tools can automate extraction and migration of products, bundles, attributes, and price books, but catalog rationalization and attribute taxonomy decisions still require review. The recommended iterative process scans the source org, maps fields and relationships, migrates a representative sample into a full-copy sandbox, validates results, fixes failures, and then scales to larger datasets. Reconciliation checks should cover record counts, relationship integrity, and downstream pricing and configuration behavior."],connections:[]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Assign Product Catalog Management permission sets through user permission-set assignments and configure required record sharing.","Create catalogs, category hierarchies, Dynamic Attributes, and Product Classifications.","Create simple products, bundled products, product variants, qualification rules, and Product Selling Models.","Connect bundle components through ProductRelatedComponent records and organize them with ProductComponentGroup records.","Assign products to catalog categories and configure Product Discovery search, qualification, pricing, and Guided Product Selection.","Assign products to price books and use catalog products in Product Configurator for downstream bundle configuration."],
  connections: [{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Products assigned to price books"},{"planet":"configurator","desc":"Product bundles use catalog products"},{"planet":"setup","desc":"Catalog permissions managed in settings"}]
},

pricing: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Pricing",
  icon: "\u{1F4B2}",
  color: "#22c55e",
  description: "Pricing in Revenue Cloud controls how catalog products are priced across transaction and sales-channel contexts. Price Books supply base prices, while Pricing Procedures orchestrate Pricing Elements for discounts, adjustments, proration, aggregation, contract pricing, and other calculations. Promotions apply adjustments through the pricing engine, and Rate Management extends pricing for usage products. Price Waterfall records explain the sequence of pricing outcomes for validation and troubleshooting.",
  components: [
    {id:"price-books",name:"Price Books",icon:"\u{1F4D5}",desc:"Price Books centralize the base or list selling prices used to price products in Revenue Cloud. Products are associated with a price book through entries that identify the product, price book, unit price, currency, and activation state. These records provide the list-price inputs that pricing procedures use when calculating transaction line prices.",tags:["Pricebook2","PricebookEntry","PriceBookAssignment"],docs:["A price book represents a centralized collection of product selling prices. Pricebook2 records identify the price book and indicate whether it is active or standard. Products from the catalog are assigned to price books through PricebookEntry records, enabling Revenue Cloud to retrieve a relevant base price during pricing.","Each PricebookEntry connects a product and price book through Product2Id and Pricebook2Id. UnitPrice records the selling price, while CurrencyIsoCode identifies its currency. UseStandardPrice and IsActive control whether the entry uses the standard price and whether it is available for pricing."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.pricing_salesforce_pricing.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Price books contain entries for catalog products"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Price books provide base prices for quote and order lines"}]},
    {id:"pricing-procedures",name:"Pricing Procedures",icon:"\u{2699}",desc:"Pricing Procedures define the orchestration or pricing policy used to price products and services in Revenue Cloud. Each procedure arranges Pricing Elements that perform calculations such as list pricing, discounting, proration, aggregation, and custom logic. Transaction Management and its APIs use pricing procedures to calculate quote and order line prices consistently.",tags:["PricingProcedure","PricingProcedureStep","PricingCalculation"],docs:["A Pricing Procedure defines how Revenue Cloud calculates prices for a particular business context. Administrators compose the procedure from Pricing Elements and can establish List Groups with conditions that determine which elements execute. Sales Transaction Type can be used to trigger different pricing procedures for quote or order pricing.","Pricing procedures exchange application data through context definitions. To provide efficient data access for procedures used by Transaction Management and Transaction Management APIs, administrators create or extend the applicable context definitions. Pricing Action extends this model by allowing custom context definitions and pricing procedures to calculate prices for Salesforce objects.","Pricing Simulation lets administrators run an orchestration against real data before relying on it operationally. They can inspect the resulting Price Waterfall to validate expected prices and discounts. Parallel Element Execution can process elements concurrently to improve the performance of pricing APIs and their simulations."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Pricing procedures calculate prices during quoting"},{"planet":"rates","desc":"Pricing procedures can invoke rate calculations for usage products"}]},
    {id:"price-adjustments",name:"Price Adjustments",icon:"\u{2702}",desc:"Price Adjustments modify product prices according to defined business conditions in Revenue Cloud. Price Adjustment Schedules group volume-based, attribute-based, bundle-based, or custom adjustments for specified products and effective periods. Adjustment tiers define ordered bounds and values for tiered or volume-driven calculations, while promotions can apply additional adjustments through the pricing engine.",tags:["PriceAdjustmentSchedule","PriceAdjustmentTier","PriceAdjustment"],docs:["Price Adjustment Schedules organize reusable adjustments and identify their adjustment type and method. A schedule can contain PriceAdjustmentTier records that define LowerBound, UpperBound, AdjustmentValue, TierType, and Sequence. The sequence establishes the order of the tiers associated with the schedule.","Revenue Cloud provides Pricing Elements for several adjustment scenarios. Volume Discount calculates volume-based adjustments, while Tiered Discount calculates tiered, or slab-based, adjustments. Attribute-Based Price and Bundle-Based Price apply discounts when a qualifying product meets the applicable eligibility criteria.","Runtime adjustments can also participate in pricing execution. Manual Discount applies percentage, amount, or override adjustments entered for a transaction rather than established during the definition phase. The Promotion element evaluates and applies promotion-based discounts to transaction lines during the pricing process."],connections:[{"planet":"promotions","desc":"Promotions create adjustment entries during pricing"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Adjustments modify line item prices on quotes and orders"}]},
    {id:"price-waterfall",name:"Price Waterfall",icon:"\u{1F4CA}",desc:"Price Waterfall provides detailed information about the outcome of pricing execution for a Revenue Cloud transaction. It presents the sequence through which products or services were priced, including the discounts and adjustments applied along the way. Administrators can use the waterfall during Pricing Simulation and operational troubleshooting to understand a calculated result.",tags:["PriceWaterfallItem","PriceWaterfallStep"],docs:["The Price Waterfall explains pricing execution step by step rather than showing only the final price. It exposes how list price, discounts, adjustments, and other calculations contributed to the transaction result. This detail helps users validate the expected price and discount for each product.","Pricing Simulation produces a waterfall that administrators can review while testing a Pricing Procedure with real data. Revenue Cloud setup includes the enablePricingWaterfall and enablePricingWaterfallPersistence settings for enabling the waterfall and its persistence. The Pricing Operations Console uses price logs to help troubleshoot and resolve issues caused by pricing APIs."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Price waterfall details appear on quote and order lines"},{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Waterfall data feeds pricing analytics and margin reports"}]},
    {id:"price-elements",name:"Price Elements",icon:"\u{1F9E9}",desc:"Price Elements are the composable structures within a Pricing Procedure that implement individual pricing solutions in Revenue Cloud. Administrators arrange these elements to address list pricing, discounts, proration, aggregation, propagation, and other business requirements. Their ordered execution produces transaction prices and the corresponding Price Waterfall.",tags:["PriceElement","PriceElementType","PricingFormula"],docs:["Get List Price and List Price calculate a product's base price within a Pricing Procedure. Subscription Pricing calculates a subscription price while considering the proration value. Manual Discount, Volume Discount, Tiered Discount, Attribute-Based Price, and Bundle-Based Price then support distinct adjustment scenarios.","Other Pricing Elements support more specialized calculations and data handling. Aggregate Price combines prices for multiple products according to defined rules, while Derived Pricing calculates a product's price from products, headers, assets, or historical prices. Formula Pricing applies a custom pricing formula, and Assignment Element assigns values from one context tag to another.","Price Adjustment Matrix executes custom pricing rules for a specific product, and Price Revision applies a price revision policy through formulas based on price adjustments. Pricing Propagation applies discounts, margins, and custom calculations from groups to line items and rolls the results into totals. Procedure Output Resolution selects the best price when a pricing rule produces multiple outcomes."],connections:[{"planet":"procedures","desc":"Price elements can invoke expression sets for complex calculations"}]},
    {id:"contract-pricing",name:"Contract Pricing",icon:"\u{1F4DD}",desc:"Contract Pricing captures and enforces customer-specific pricing and discount agreements for future purchases in Revenue Cloud. Contract-Based Pricing determines a transaction price from a contract associated with the product. It can also use contract volume tiers or cumulative quantities to apply negotiated prices and discounts.",tags:["ContractPrice","ContractPriceEntry","NegotiatedPrice"],docs:["Contract-Based Pricing is a Pricing Element that determines prices from a specific contract associated with a product in a transaction. The element applies this pricing only when the Use Contract-based Pricing option is enabled. This approach lets Revenue Cloud enforce customer-specific agreements during later purchases.","The Contract Pricing Schedule component on the Contract page provides a consolidated view of the contract prices in a pricing contract. Contract Pricing Volume Tiers define the prices or discounts a customer receives at different sales volumes. Each tier is directly associated with a contract item price record on the contract record page.","Cumulative Quantity Contract Pricing calculates contract-based volume or tier pricing from the aggregate quantity purchased across multiple line items and transactions. This supports agreements in which eligibility depends on combined purchasing activity instead of the quantity of a single transaction line. Cumulative Quantity Pricing can include historical purchases when generating volume and tier-based discounts."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Contract prices override list prices during quoting"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Contract pricing flows through to billing schedules"}]},
    {id:"ai-pricing",name:"AI-Powered Pricing",icon:"\u{1F916}",desc:"Revenue Cloud documentation describes Agentforce as a way to automate revenue-management tasks and allow sales reps to focus on customer relationships. The supplied documentation does not define a dedicated AI pricing suggestion mechanism or document AI-generated price recommendations. Pricing calculations remain documented through Pricing Procedures, Pricing Elements, pricing APIs, and Pricing Simulation.",tags:["AIPricingRecommendation","PricingInsight","PricingModel"],docs:["Agentforce support in Revenue Cloud is presented as platform-level task automation across the revenue lifecycle. The documentation identifies agent templates and setup considerations, but it does not specify an AI pricing model, recommendation workflow, or pricing-suggestion configuration. Administrators should therefore rely on the documented pricing mechanisms for price calculation and validation."],connections:[{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"AI pricing models use revenue analytics data"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"AI suggestions appear during quote creation"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Assign catalog products to active Price Books through PricebookEntry records.","Define Price Adjustment Schedules and ordered PriceAdjustmentTier records for applicable products.","Assemble Pricing Elements into Pricing Procedures and map the required context data.","Invoke the applicable Pricing Procedure to calculate transaction line prices and apply contract pricing or promotions.","Extend pricing with Rate Management for usage products and inspect the Price Waterfall to validate the result."],
  connections: [{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Products assigned to price books"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Pricing procedures calculate line item prices"},{"planet":"rates","desc":"Rate management extends pricing for usage products"},{"planet":"promotions","desc":"Promotions apply adjustments via pricing engine"}]
},

rates: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Rating and Metering",
  icon: "\u{1F4CF}",
  color: "#06b6d4",
  description: "Rating and Metering extends Revenue Cloud pricing for products whose charges depend on measured consumption. Rate cards define base or tiered rates for usage resources, and rate adjustments account for quantities, tiers, commitments, negotiated terms, or other configured conditions. Rating Procedures apply these rates and adjustments in an ordered calculation that produces the final net rate and can be inspected through Rating Waterfall. Usage Management supplies consumption information to rating, and the resulting rated usage supports billing schedules and invoices for usage-based products.",
  components: [
    {id:"rate-cards",name:"Rate Cards",icon:"\u{1F4C7}",desc:"Rate Cards define the rules used to rate consumption for groups of usage resources within a product. Base rate cards establish flat per-unit rates, while Tier rate cards support adjustments based on consumption quantity. Rate card entries associate rates with a usage resource, sellable product, product selling model, unit of measure, status, and effective period. Revenue Cloud maps rate cards to price books so usage resources can be rated in the appropriate selling context.",tags:["RateCard","RateCardEntry","RateTier"],docs:["Rate Cards support consumption-based pricing for usage resources granted with sellable products. A rate card has a type, such as Base or Tier, and an effective date range. Base rate cards define flat rates for resource consumption, while Tier rate cards define rate adjustments based on the quantity consumed. Specific rates can apply when customers consume a resource beyond its granted limits or period.","Rate card entries provide the rates and adjustments used to calculate and control the final net rate of each usage resource. A Base rate card entry defines the per-unit cost or charge for consumed units. A Tier rate card entry defines consumption tiers and a rate for each tier, enabling adjusted rates for customers with different consumption levels. Revenue Cloud supports defining this pricing in standard currency or tokens.","A Price Book Rate Card record maps the price book related to a sellable product to a rate card related to the corresponding usage resource. Administrators create a separate mapping for each applicable rate card type, such as Base or Tier. Rating Discovery Procedures can then fetch rate cards, rate card entries, and rate adjustments for multiple sellable products and usage resources related to a price book. The retrieved rate information supports usage-based quotes through Quote and Order Capture and Asset Lifecycle."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.rm_rate_management.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"usage","desc":"Rate cards are applied to usage records for charge calculation"},{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Rate cards extend the pricing framework for consumption products"}]},
    {id:"rating-procedures",name:"Rating Procedures",icon:"\u{2699}",desc:"Rating Procedures are customizable, ordered stacks of rating elements that calculate the final net rate of a usage resource. Each element represents a calculation step and can call an appropriate lookup table. Procedures can combine base rates, quantity-based adjustments, negotiated rates, formulas, assignments, rounding, and other supported rating elements. Rating Waterfall exposes the sequence and result of rating decisions.",tags:["RatingProcedure","RatingProcedureStep","RatingCalculation"],docs:["A new Rating Procedure begins as a blank procedure to which designers add rating elements in the required order. Rating Setting maps commonly used variables to context tags, and elements such as Base Rate, Volume-Based Rate Discount, Tier-Based Rate Discount, and Commitment Rate Adjustment perform specific calculations. Formula-Based Rating performs mathematical calculations, while Assignment changes context tag values and Rounding Values applies rounding rules. Stop Rating can end execution for a particular line item.","Building a procedure requires Rate Management Design Time user permission, applicable lookup tables, and enabled context definitions. Revenue Cloud provides the RateManagementContext predefined context definition for mapping the Usage Ratable Summary object. Designers can use predefined lookup tables for non-negotiable and negotiable rates or create custom lookup tables. Constants provide fixed values for variables that don't have context tags.","In the Rating Procedure builder, designers add elements, map variables to context tags, assign ranks, and select which results to include in the output. They can simulate a procedure with Simplified field inputs or Advanced JSON inputs and verify the calculations before activation. Rating Waterfall displays each step of the calculation, and an enabled version with the highest matching rank is selected when multiple versions match. Negotiable Rating Procedure processing can use detailed usage events captured by Usage Management to apply negotiated rates and discounts."],connections:[{"planet":"usage","desc":"Rating procedures process usage records into charges"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Rated charges flow into billing for invoicing"}]},
    {id:"rate-adjustments",name:"Rate Adjustments",icon:"\u{1F4C9}",desc:"Rate Adjustments modify usage-resource rates according to configured consumption or business conditions. Revenue Cloud supports volume-based, tier-based, attribute-based, commitment-based, manual, and custom-condition adjustments through rating elements and lookup tables. Adjustments can apply discounts, surcharges, or overrides as supported by the selected element and configuration. Negotiated adjustments can be retrieved from asset or binding-object rate information.",tags:["RateAdjustment","RateAdjustmentRule","CommittedUseDiscount"],docs:["Volume-Based Rate Discount calculates a usage-resource rate from discounts configured for consumed quantities. Tier-Based Rate Discount determines the discount based on the quantity consumed across defined tiers. Rate Adjustment Matrix calculates a rate from discounts configured for a set of custom conditions, and Commitment Rate Adjustment applies adjustments and calculates rates for commitment-based products. These elements use mapped context tags and their applicable lookup tables during procedure execution.","Non-negotiable volume and tier calculations use the Rate Adjustment by Volume Entries 2 and Rate Adjustment by Tier Entries 2 lookup tables. Negotiable calculations can use Volume-based Rate Adjustment by Rate Card Entry ID and Tier-based Rate Adjustment by Rate Card Entry ID. Negotiated Volume-Based Rate Adjustment and Negotiated Tier-Based Rate Adjustment can retrieve adjustments associated with assets or binding objects. The Negotiated Rate Card Entries element must precede negotiated adjustment elements when its Rate Card Entry ID output is required as their input.","Manual Rate Discount supports number, percentage, and currency adjustment types. The selected type determines how Revenue Cloud performs the calculation, such as applying a fixed amount or a percentage discount. Before quantity-based or manual discounts are calculated, Rating Setting must be the first procedure element, and the procedure must establish a base rate or use an Override adjustment type. Designers simulate the resulting calculation and review it in Waterfall View before activating the procedure."],connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Rate adjustments interact with pricing procedures"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Negotiated rate adjustments are captured during deal creation"}]},
    {id:"rate-policies",name:"Rate Policies",icon:"\u{1F4DC}",desc:"Rate Policies are part of the Revenue Cloud data model for end-to-end usage management. The documented Rate Management entities include Rating Frequency Policy alongside rate cards, usage resources, rating requests, and Expression Sets. Runtime access to Rating Frequency Policy records is controlled through Revenue Cloud record sharing and user permissions. These controls let runtime users access policy data created during design-time administration.",tags:["RatePolicy","RatePolicyRule","RatingBehavior"],docs:["The Usage Management data model includes policies together with rate cards, rate adjustments, product definitions, and runtime objects for usage-based products and usage resources. Rate Management identifies Rating Frequency Policy as one of the entities that runtime users may need to access. Policy records therefore participate in the shared data used by the consumption and rating model.","Administrators configure record sharing for Rating Frequency Policy according to their security requirements. Revenue Cloud supports using Organization-Wide Defaults or Sharing Rules for this access, with Public ReadOnly access provided as an example. Runtime users also receive the Rate Management Run Time User permission set to obtain Read access to relevant objects. This setup allows runtime processing to access records created by rating designers, managers, or catalog administrators."],connections:[{"planet":"billing","desc":"Rate policies influence how rated charges appear on invoices"},{"planet":"usage","desc":"Policies govern overage and rollover behavior for usage"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Configure a usage-based sellable product and associate its usage resources.","Create Base or Tier rate cards and define their effective rate card entries.","Map each applicable rate card to the product's price book with a Price Book Rate Card record.","Capture and process consumption events through Usage Management.","Execute the ordered Rating Procedure to apply rates and adjustments to the usage resource.","Use the rated consumption results in usage-based billing schedules and invoices."],
  connections: [{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Rate management extends pricing for usage products"},{"planet":"usage","desc":"Usage resources rated using rate cards"}]
},

configurator: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Product Configurator",
  icon: "\u{1F527}",
  color: "#14b8a6",
  description: "Product Configurator in Revenue Cloud helps users customize catalog products, individual attributes, and multilevel product bundles for customer and business requirements. Product Configurator flows and flow screen components provide the run-time experience for selecting components, quantities, options, and attributes, while Instant Pricing can update prices after configuration changes. Business Rules Engine supports simple configuration rules, and Constraint Rules Engine uses constraint models and CML to validate large and complex configurations and apply rule outcomes. Valid configured products are saved to quotes and orders for transaction processing.",
  components: [
    {id:"visual-builder",name:"Visual Builder",icon:"\u{1F3A8}",desc:"Visual Builder is a point-and-click interface within Constraint Builder for defining constraints and rules in Revenue Cloud. Designers can use it instead of working directly with Constraint Modeling Language (CML) code. Visual Builder supports several constraint and rule types for products and product bundles, and it generates the corresponding CML representation. Designers can switch between Visual Builder and CML Editor while developing a constraint model.",tags:["ProductConfigurationSetup","ConfigurationBuilder","ConfigurationView"],docs:["Visual Builder is one of the two interfaces available in Constraint Builder. It provides point-and-click tools for defining basic logic constraints, conditional logic constraints, dynamic message rules, require rules, exclude rules, hide/disable rules, and preference rules. Designers build expressions by selecting a left-side element, an operator, and a right-side element or static value. Multiple expressions can be combined with grouping logic that uses AND, OR, or XOR operators.","Constraints and rules created in Visual Builder are represented as CML code in CML Editor. Designers can define an initial model visually, switch to CML Editor to inspect or extend the generated code, and return to Visual Builder as needed. Some constraints and rules authored directly in CML Editor aren't editable in Visual Builder because they aren't represented by its point-and-click tools. When a bundle is imported through Visual Builder, its product component groups and cardinality are imported from Product Catalog Management, although constraints and rules can't be defined at the group level."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.product_configurator_introduction.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Configured products are added to quotes and orders"},{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Visual builder loads products and bundles from the catalog"}]},
    {id:"configuration-rules",name:"Configuration Rules",icon:"\u{1F4CF}",desc:"Configuration Rules in Revenue Cloud govern product selection, attributes, quantities, compatibility, and validation during configuration. Business Rules Engine supports simple product configuration rules, while Constraint Rules Engine supports large and complex configurations through constraint models and CML. Rule scope can apply to a product, a bundle, or an entire transaction. The transaction processing type on a quote or order determines which configured rules engine processes the configuration.",tags:["ProductConfigurationRule","RuleCondition","RuleAction"],docs:["Business Rules Engine configuration rules contain conditions and actions that guide sales reps toward valid product selections. Depending on rule scope, supported actions include Auto-Add, Auto-Remove, Disable Product, Disable Attribute Value, Exclude, Hide Product, Hide Attribute, Hide Attribute Value, Require, Set Attribute, Set Quantity, Set Default Attribute, Set Default Attribute Value, Set Default Product, and Validate. Rules can apply to a single product, all child products within a root bundle, or all line items in a quote or order. Designers can also assign a sequence so that lower-numbered rules run first when multiple rules trigger together.","Constraint Rules Engine uses constraint models to validate large and complex configurations. Constraint Builder provides Visual Builder and CML Editor interfaces for authoring the model, and the engine compiles the CML into a constraint model that enforces the specified relationships, variables, constraints, and rules. Designers can activate a constraint model after defining its associations with catalog products, product classifications, or product component groups. Constraint Rules Engine can also run Hide/Disable, Message, and Recommend rules through the Run Config Rules invocable action.","Revenue Cloud uses Transaction Processing Type records to select a rules engine. StandardConfigurator identifies Business Rules Engine, while AdvancedConfigurator identifies Constraint Rules Engine. When both engines are enabled, the Transaction Type field on a quote or order and its associated Transaction Processing Type record determine which engine validates the product configuration. Business Rules Engine requires an active rule library, while Constraint Rules Engine requires its context definitions, custom status field, and transaction processing setup."],connections:[{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Rules reference products and attributes from the catalog"}]},
    {id:"bundle-configuration",name:"Bundle Configuration",icon:"\u{1F4E6}",desc:"Bundle Configuration in Revenue Cloud supports static and configurable product bundles built from catalog products. Static bundles can't be changed at run time, while configurable bundles allow users to select child products, change permitted quantities, and configure attributes. Both bundle types can contain multilevel product structures, subject to their distinct component rules. Product Configurator presents eligible bundle components and updates configuration pricing through Instant Pricing or a manual price update.",tags:["ProductBundle","BundleOption","OptionGroup","BundleComponent"],docs:["Designers create a bundled product by setting Product Type to Bundle and using Configure During Sale to determine whether the bundle is static or configurable. The Structure tab is used to build the hierarchy. A static product bundle can contain static simple products or static bundled products, and its optional groups can contain only multiple product components. Product classification components aren't supported within static bundle groups.","Groups are mandatory for configurable product bundles, and child components must be added under groups rather than directly under the root product. A configurable bundle group can contain multiple product components or one product classification component. Adding a product classification component makes products based on that classification available dynamically at run time. Configurable bundles can include static or configurable simple products and static or configurable bundled products.","Bundle configuration supports local cardinality and group cardinality. Local cardinality controls whether a component is required or included by default, whether its quantity can change, and its default, minimum, and maximum quantities. Group cardinality controls the minimum and maximum number of distinct child components that users can select. Designers can also override component attributes in the context of a bundle without changing the attributes of the underlying catalog product.","At run time, users can configure a bundled product from a quote or order, update bundle and component quantities, select attributes and options, and dynamically add eligible products. Configuration changes to a child product are represented on the corresponding transaction line. Instant Pricing updates the Summary component after configuration changes, while Product Validation checks qualification, Product Catalog Management validation, and configuration rules. Valid configurations can then be saved with the quote or order."],connections:[{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Bundles are composed of products from the catalog"},{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Bundle pricing rules interact with the pricing engine"}]},
    {id:"exclude-preference-rules",name:"Exclude and Preference Rules",icon:"\u{26D4}",desc:"Exclude and Preference Rules are Constraint Rules Engine rule types used in Revenue Cloud constraint models. An Exclude Rule automatically removes a specified type from a relationship when its condition is met. A Preference Rule encourages the constraint solver to satisfy a condition without enforcing it when the condition can't be met. These rules are distinct from disable rules and recommendation rules used to control product availability and suggestions.",tags:["ExclusionRule","PreferenceRule","ProductCompatibility"],docs:["An Exclude Rule defines one or more conditions and identifies the item to exclude when those conditions are true. The rule automatically removes the specified type from a relationship, such as excluding a product from a bundle configuration. Designers can create the rule with point-and-click expressions in Visual Builder or define it directly in CML Editor. Exclusion is an actionable configuration outcome rather than a recommendation or a disabled visual state.","A Preference Rule defines conditions and a preferred outcome for the constraint solver. The solver attempts to satisfy the preferred condition, but it can override that condition if user input makes the preference impossible to meet. Designers can provide an optional run-time message when the input violates the preferred outcome. This behavior allows configuration to continue instead of enforcing the preference as a mandatory constraint."],connections:[{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Exclusion rules reference product compatibility data"}]},
    {id:"dynamic-messages",name:"Dynamic Messages",icon:"\u{1F4AC}",desc:"Dynamic Messages in Revenue Cloud provide contextual information, warnings, and errors while users configure products. Constraint Rules Engine supports a dynamic message rule that displays a message when specified conditions are true. Business Rules Engine actions can also include messages that help sales reps correct product selections. Product Configurator presents configuration messages through its Messages component and related validation interfaces.",tags:["ConfigurationMessage","MessageCondition","ValidationMessage"],docs:["In Visual Builder, a dynamic message rule contains one or more expressions that determine when a message appears. The designer enters the run-time message and assigns an Info, Warning, or Error severity. An informational message doesn't require user action, a warning blocks the next step on the configuration page until the issue is addressed, and an error blocks the current configuration task until the error is fixed. In Transaction Line Editor, however, an error message doesn't prevent the user from changing and saving the quote.","The Product Configurator Messages component renders Error, Warning, and Info messages in an expandable accordion. It sorts messages by severity, shows count badges, and lets users filter by severity. The Error Component also serves as a central location for errors, warnings, and informational messages associated with the product being configured. Message rules can therefore explain configuration outcomes without replacing the constraints or actionable rules that produced them.","Constraint model messages can be prepared for translation by associating them with custom labels. The custom labels replace static message text in CML and can be translated through the Salesforce translation settings. At run time, the translated message is selected according to the sales rep's locale and the languages configured in the org. Messages without an associated custom label remain untranslated."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Configuration messages appear during quote line editing"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Select catalog products and define static or configurable bundle structures.","Assign attributes, cardinality, configuration flows, and the applicable rules engine.","Configure components, quantities, options, and attributes in Product Configurator.","Evaluate configuration rules and constraints, display messages, and update pricing.","Validate and save the configured products to quotes or orders.","Process the configured products through the quote and order lifecycle."],
  connections: [{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Product bundles use catalog products"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Configured products flow into quotes and orders"}]
},

promotions: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Promotions",
  icon: "\u{1F3AF}",
  color: "#84cc16",
  description: "Promotions in Revenue Cloud let pricing designers offer percentage or amount discounts on simple products, bundled products, and product categories. Eligibility can depend on transaction context, including product selling models, products, categories, customer groups, dates, coupons, currencies, and configured sales channels. Sales reps, partners, and customers can view or apply eligible promotions while managing quotes, orders, and assets. The Promotion Execution element evaluates promotions and applies their adjustments through the pricing engine.",
  components: [
    {id:"promotion-management",name:"Promotion Management",icon:"\u{1F4CB}",desc:"Promotion Management provides the setup and design experience for creating and activating promotions in Revenue Cloud. Pricing designers create promotions by opening Promotions & Offers from the App Launcher and clicking New Promotion. Promotions can provide percentage or amount discounts for simple products, bundled products, and product categories. Sales reps, partners, and customers can view or apply eligible promotions while working with quotes, orders, and assets.",tags:["Promotion","PromotionSegment","PromotionTarget"],docs:["To create a promotion, open Promotions & Offers from the App Launcher and click New Promotion. Enter the primary promotion details, define eligibility, and select Get Discount + Rewards on X (Based on Selling Model). Configure the priority, rule name, products or categories, product selling models, and discount values. Review the configuration and activate the promotion when it is ready for use.","Promotions can be automatic or manual. Revenue Cloud automatically applies an automatic promotion to eligible transaction lines, while a manual promotion requires an active coupon and is applied by a user. Eligible promotions can appear in Browse Catalogs, Product Configurator, and the Transaction Line Editor. Managed Assets also show promotions previously applied to an asset.","Promotion availability depends on the transaction context and the promotion configuration. Browse Catalogs shows product and category promotions, while Product Configurator shows eligible promotions for the root product of a bundle. The Transaction Line Editor side panel can show eligible promotions for transaction lines, including child products. Each applied promotion creates an Asset Action Source Price Adjustment record when the transaction is reflected in an asset. "],connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Promotions create price adjustments during pricing calculations"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Active promotions apply to qualifying quotes and orders"}]},
    {id:"promotion-rules",name:"Promotion Rules",icon:"\u{1F4CF}",desc:"Promotion Rules define when a Revenue Cloud promotion is eligible and how multiple eligible promotions are evaluated. Rules can use products, categories, product selling models, customer groups, coupons, configured channels, currency, and transaction dates. Bundle promotions can apply to the root product, child products, or both. A rule library maps the transaction context tags used by promotion eligibility and execution.",tags:["PromotionRule","PromotionCondition","PromotionRuleGroup"],docs:["Pricing designers define promotion eligibility while creating a promotion in Promotions & Offers. They can select customer groups, configure products or categories by product selling model, and optionally restrict the promotion to configured channels. Turning on coupons makes the promotion manual and requires an active coupon; otherwise, Revenue Cloud applies the promotion automatically to eligible lines. Promotions for child products are available only when customers purchase those products as part of the configured bundle.","Revenue Cloud supports Stacked and First Eligible Promotion evaluation methods for line rewards. Stacked calculates eligible promotion discounts independently and then aggregates them. First Eligible Promotion calculates only the first eligible promotion according to priority. Pricing designers select the method through Configure Evaluation and Execution for the Revenue Cloud rule library.","Promotion rules have documented restrictions. Promotions can't be created for usage-based products, although a usage-based product can receive a category promotion. Promotions don't apply to products with derived pricing or to quotes and orders that contain ramp deals for groups. If a product belongs to multiple categories, promotion evaluation can select one of those categories, which can produce inconsistent pricing outcomes."],connections:[{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Promotion rules reference products and categories from the catalog"}]},
    {id:"promotional-pricing",name:"Promotional Pricing",icon:"\u{1F4B0}",desc:"Promotional Pricing applies promotion discounts during Revenue Cloud price calculation. The Promotion Execution element evaluates applicable promotions for transaction lines and updates their Net Unit Price and subtotal. It connects Global Promotions Management with Revenue Cloud pricing procedures. Applied adjustments are processed through the pricing engine and can be inspected in the price waterfall.",tags:["PromotionalPrice","PromotionBenefit","PromotionDiscount"],docs:["To enable promotional pricing, add the Promotion Execution element to the default transaction pricing procedure. Place it in a List Group after bundle-based adjustments, select the Revenue Cloud rule library, and set Promotion Scope to Line. Map ItemNetTotalPrice, LineItemQuantity, and NetUnitPrice as inputs. Map NetUnitPrice and ItemNetTotalPrice as outputs, then save and activate the pricing procedure.","The rule library supplies the context used during promotion evaluation. Its mappings include ProductSellingModel, Account or BillToContact, LineItem, Product, CurrencyIsoCode, ProductCategory, StartDate, coupon code, root bundle product, and the promotion evaluation configuration. An optional Channel mapping supports channel-based eligibility after channels are configured. Product Discovery can also be customized to evaluate promotions using transaction currency instead of account currency.","Price Waterfall and Price Waterfall Persistence must be enabled before Promotions for Transactions is turned on. Users can hover over a transaction line's Net Unit Price to review promotion-related calculations in the price waterfall. The Promotion Execution element can't be used in the same pricing procedure as Derived Price or Price Propagation. If Promotions for Transactions is turned off, the Promotion Execution element must be removed from the pricing procedure."],connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Promotional pricing feeds into the price adjustment framework"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Promotional benefits appear as line-level discounts on quotes"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Enable Promotions for Transactions and configure the Revenue Cloud rule library.","Create a promotion from Promotions & Offers by clicking New Promotion.","Define targets, eligibility criteria, discounts, priority, and evaluation behavior.","Activate the promotion and add eligible products to a quote or order.","Evaluate applicable promotions with the Promotion Execution element.","Apply the resulting adjustments through the pricing engine and review the price waterfall."],
  connections: [{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Promotions apply adjustments via pricing engine"}]
},

transactions: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Quotes and Orders",
  icon: "\u{1F4DD}",
  color: "#4d8bff",
  description: "Quotes and Orders is the Revenue Cloud transactional domain for creating and managing quotes, orders, contracts, and their line items. Configured products flow into standard Quote and Order records, where pricing procedures calculate prices and the line editors support product, group, discount, and transaction management. The domain supports advanced CSV file imports through Data Processing Engine, approvals, quote-to-order conversion, amendments, renewals, cancellations, and multi-period Ramp Deals; Agentforce assists with creating and managing quotes. Order activation can create assets and billing schedules, while submitted orders can be decomposed by Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator into fulfillment order line items and fulfillment plans.",
  components: [
    {id:"quote-management",name:"Quote Management",icon:"\u{1F4C4}",desc:"Quote Management supports the creation and management of Revenue Cloud quotes as preliminary offers for products, services, and pricing. Sales reps can add configured catalog products, calculate prices with pricing procedures, synchronize quotes with opportunities, and route quotes through approval workflows. Quotes can be revised, cloned, converted to contracts or orders, and managed with Agentforce AI agents. Quote records and quote line items use the standard Salesforce data model.",tags:["Quote","QuoteVersion","QuoteDocument","QuoteLineItem"],docs:["Sales reps can create a quote from an opportunity or from the Quotes tab, then enter details such as the account, opportunity, and status. Product Discovery provides Browse Catalogs for finding eligible products, selecting buying options, and adding configured products to the quote. Pricing procedures calculate quote line prices, discounts, and totals, while the price waterfall explains the adjustments used to determine a line's price. Advanced Approvals can route quotes through sequential or parallel approval chains before the transaction progresses.","Quote synchronization connects quote line items to products on the related opportunity. Because opportunities don't support bundle structures or product attributes, bundled products are represented as separate opportunity products and selected attributes aren't copied. Sales reps can use Start Sync and Stop Sync on the quote to control this one-way synchronization. Accepted quotes can be converted to orders, and quote or order data can also be used to create contracts.","Agentforce provides AI agent-assisted quoting through the Revenue Quote Management template. Its Quote Management subagent can create initial, amendment, and renewal quotes, add or update quote line items, apply discounts, and update quote details through conversational requests. The documented assistance applies to creating and managing quotes, not to creating orders. Users can still create and manage orders through Transaction Management features and APIs."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.qocal_quote_and_order_capture.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"approvals","desc":"Quotes are submitted through approval workflows"},{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Accepted quotes convert into orders for fulfillment"}]},
    {id:"order-capture",name:"Order Capture",icon:"\u{1F6D2}",desc:"Order Capture manages Revenue Cloud orders and their order products as formal records of products, services, quantities, and pricing. Users can create orders directly or convert accepted quotes into one or more draft orders. Pricing procedures and approval workflows support order processing before activation. Order activation can create assets and billing schedules, while order submission to Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator begins decomposition and fulfillment.",tags:["Order","OrderItem","OrderAction","OrderSummary"],docs:["Users can create an order directly from the Orders tab or generate one from an accepted quote. Quote-to-order conversion copies product, quantity, pricing, and tax information into the order, and Advanced Order Creation From Quote can split a quote into orders by group or supported quote line field. Users can also browse catalogs, add configured products, and calculate prices within an order. Revenue Cloud APIs support order creation, price calculation, and order placement.","An order has Draft or Activated status, and an activated order can't be edited. When an order is activated, Revenue Cloud can create assets for assetizable order items through the configured assetization flow. Revenue Management Billing creates a billing schedule for each order item when the order is activated. These activation outcomes don't require the order to be contracted.","Order fulfillment is initiated separately by submitting the order to Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator. When an order is submitted, Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator decomposes commercial order line items into fulfillment order line items and composes a fulfillment plan. Fulfillment steps can then coordinate automated tasks, manual tasks, callouts, milestones, and dependencies. Submission, rather than contracting, is the documented trigger for decomposition."],connections:[{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Orders are decomposed into fulfillment plans"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Orders trigger billing schedule creation"},{"planet":"approvals","desc":"Orders may require approval before activation"}]},
    {id:"line-editor",name:"Line Editor",icon:"\u{1F4DD}",desc:"Line Editor provides a centralized workspace for managing product and pricing lines on Revenue Cloud quotes and orders. Revenue Cloud supports the Transaction Line Editor and recommends the Sales Transaction Line Editor for its streamlined single-grid layout and improved performance at scale. Users can edit lines, organize them into groups, perform bulk actions, and inspect pricing details. The editors integrate with Product Discovery, Product Configurator, pricing procedures, and transaction validation.",tags:["QuoteLineItem","OrderItem","LineEditorConfig"],docs:["The line editors appear as Quote Line Items on quote pages and Order Products on order pages. Users can add products through Browse Catalogs or the product quick-add search, save transient lines, and open Product Configurator for configurable products. Inline editing supports changes such as quantity and discounts, while bulk actions can update or delete multiple selected items. The side panel can show line details and product attributes configured for display.","The Sales Transaction Line Editor supports nested groups, group ramps, filtering, sorting, and bulk actions across lines and groups. Administrators select the displayed line, group, and related-record fields in Lightning App Builder and can configure standalone, grouped, or dropdown action buttons. Standard group and line fields can merge into a single column, and supported adjustment fields can appear in the Adjustment Type column. Custom fields must be added to the sales transaction context definition for edits to persist.","Pricing can run when users save changes, through Reprice All, or through Instant Pricing when enabled. Reprice All performs a full pricing recalculation but doesn't reevaluate product configuration rules, while Refresh Prices refreshes pricing, configuration, and taxes. Users can hover over Net Unit Price to view the price waterfall and inspect adjustments such as discounts, bundle pricing, contract pricing, or derived pricing. The Transaction Line Progress Indicator shows processing progress, informational messages, and errors."],connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Line editor triggers pricing recalculation on changes"},{"planet":"configurator","desc":"Complex products open the configurator from the line editor"}]},
    {id:"amendments-renewals",name:"Amendments and Renewals",icon:"\u{1F504}",desc:"Amendments and Renewals manage changes to Revenue Cloud assets after an initial order is activated and assetized. Sales reps can amend quantities, fields, prices, attributes, and eligible subscription dates; renew assets for another term; or cancel assets that are no longer required. Contract pricing, cotermination, future-dated changes, rollbacks, transfers, and specialized amendment types are supported subject to documented constraints. Updated orders must be assetized to apply lifecycle changes to the asset record and its history.",tags:["Amendment","Renewal","ContractAmendment","RenewalQuote"],docs:["The Managed Asset Viewer on account and contract pages provides actions to amend, renew, or cancel eligible assets. An amendment can change quantities and supported fields, while a price-only amendment applies a cancel-and-reprice treatment for the remaining term. Renewals use renewal terms from the asset or associated contract, and contract-based pricing can carry negotiated prices or discounts into amendment and renewal quotes. Cancellations create a quote or order that terminates the selected asset as of the specified date.","Revenue Cloud records lifecycle history through Asset Action, Asset Action Source, and Asset State Period records. After users complete the amendment, renewal, or cancellation transaction, an order is created and activated, and the assetization flow updates the affected assets. Negative quantity changes can use a Last In First Out strategy when an asset includes quantities from multiple prior transactions. Future-dated transactions are represented through additional asset state periods so that changes apply at the correct time.","Additional lifecycle options address specialized scenarios. Contract cotermination aligns eligible termed subscription end dates with the contract end date, and late renewal can restart an expired eligible asset with a new term. Rollback can reverse the most recent eligible future-dated amendment or renewal, while transfer and swap operations preserve the relationship between outgoing and incoming asset changes. Product type, transaction timing, usage-based selling, ramp schedules, and fulfillment state can limit which operations are available."],connections:[{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Amendments create new fulfillment plans for changes"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Amendments generate prorated billing adjustments"}]},
    {id:"ramp-deals",name:"Ramp Deals",icon:"\u{1F4C8}",desc:"Ramp Deals represent multi-period Revenue Cloud agreements in which product quantity, price, discount, or uplift changes at defined intervals. Revenue Cloud supports ramp deals for individual lines and recommends Ramp Deals for Groups for new implementations. Group-based ramps can include multiple products, bundles, usage products, trials, prorated segments, and independent schedules. Assetization consolidates related ramped lines into assets that can be managed through supported lifecycle actions.",tags:["RampDeal","RampSegment","RampSchedule"],docs:["A ramp schedule consists of time-bounded segments, each with its own dates, quantities, prices, discounts, and uplifts. With a single ramp schedule, transaction groups form a flat sequence of segments; with Multiple Ramp Schedules Per Transaction enabled, top-level ramp schedule groups contain segment subgroups. Administrators enable groups, cloning, and Ramp Deals for Groups and add the Sales Transaction Line Editor to quote and order pages. Sales reps can create annual or custom schedules and optionally add trial or prorated segments.","Products can be added to the current segment only or to the current and subsequent segments. Configuration changes to a bundled product generally propagate to the current and later segments, while root or component quantity changes apply only to the current segment. Users can clone or delete supported segments, edit schedule names and dates, view ramp details for a line, and apply discounts or unit price uplifts by segment. All segments in one schedule use the same segment type.","Ramp Deals for Groups support usage products, allowing rates and grant quantities to vary by segment while binding-type changes propagate across the related segments. When a ramp order is assetized, Revenue Cloud creates one consolidated asset for related lines of a ramped product; ramped bundles create an asset for the root and each child product. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator can decompose submitted ramp orders and fulfill their segments according to the configured dates, quantities, and pricing. Amendments and the dedicated early-renewal flow support selected ramped-asset changes, subject to the documented ramp restrictions."],connections:[{"planet":"billing","desc":"Ramp segments trigger billing schedule changes at each transition"},{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Each ramp segment has its own pricing calculation"}]},
    {id:"quote-line-import",name:"Quote Line Item Import",icon:"\u{1F4E5}",desc:"Quote Line Item Import provides an advanced CSV file import capability for adding quote lines in bulk. Revenue Cloud uses a Data Processing Engine definition to process CSV data and create quote line items. Administrators can use the supplied template and flow or customize them for supported fields and business requirements. Users require the Advanced CSV Data Import permission set to download templates and run imports.",tags:["QuoteLineImport","ImportMapping","ImportValidation"],docs:["Administrators turn on Import Quote Line Items and select an active Data Processing Engine definition on the Revenue Settings page. Revenue Cloud supplies separate Data Processing Engine definition templates for single-currency and multicurrency organizations. The definition reads CSV rows, resolves products and product selling models, creates quote line items, and can create related records for supported static bundles and attributes. Product codes are required for products included in the import.","Users open a quote, select Import Lines, download the CSV template, populate it, upload the file, and start the import. The default template requires ProductCode, RowNumber, and Quantity, and products with selling models also require ProductSellingModelName. Imported lines can then be configured and repriced as part of the quote. Revenue Cloud suspends other actions on the quote while the import is running and records failures in Revenue Transaction Error Log records.","Implementations can create a custom CSV template, customize the Import Quote Line Items flow, and modify the Data Processing Engine definition to support mapped custom fields. Custom fields must also be represented in the extended Sales Transaction context definition so their values persist. The documented import supports initial and amendment quotes but not renewal or cancellation quotes, and usage-based products aren't supported. For bundles, importing the root product adds the bundle, after which users manually configure child products unless the Data Processing Engine definition is configured to create supported default related records."],connections:[{"planet":"procedures","desc":"Document processing engine handles import transformations"},{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Import validates product codes against the catalog"}]},
    {id:"transaction-summary",name:"Transaction Summary",icon:"\u{1F4CA}",desc:"Transaction Summary shows a concise financial snapshot of a Revenue Cloud quote or order. It surfaces selected pricing, discount, and total fields so users can verify transaction economics before processing the record. Administrators configure separate fields for the component body and footer and can expand the summary by default. The component works with the line editor to present transaction-level information alongside detailed product lines.",tags:["TransactionSummary","DealMetrics","RevenueBreakdown"],docs:["Administrators add the Transaction Summary component to quote or order record pages in Lightning App Builder. They select the fields shown in the component's body and footer, arrange their order, and choose whether the details are expanded when the page loads. The component uses fields from the underlying quote or order, providing a scannable overview of transaction totals and adjustments. Users need field-level access to the selected fields for the values to appear.","The Transaction Summary should be placed on the same tab as the Transaction Line Editor or Sales Transaction Line Editor. Its position in Lightning App Builder doesn't change the live rendering behavior, because it appears at the bottom of the page. Address data types, such as Ship To or Bill To, aren't supported in the component. Detailed line-level calculations remain available through the line editor and price waterfall."],connections:[{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Transaction metrics feed into revenue dashboards"},{"planet":"approvals","desc":"Summary metrics are evaluated by approval rules"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Create and manage a quote, optionally with Agentforce, and add configured catalog products.","Enter lines in a line editor or import quote line items through a Data Processing Engine definition.","Run pricing procedures to calculate line prices, discounts, and transaction totals.","Submit quotes or orders to the applicable approval workflows.","Convert an accepted quote to one or more orders, then activate orders to create assets and billing schedules.","Submit orders to Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator for decomposition and fulfillment-plan execution."],
  connections: [{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Pricing procedures calculate line item prices"},{"planet":"configurator","desc":"Configured products flow into quotes and orders"},{"planet":"approvals","desc":"Quotes and orders go through approval workflows"},{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Contracted orders decomposed into fulfillment plans"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Orders trigger billing schedule creation"},{"planet":"procedures","desc":"Document processing engine handles quote line imports"},{"planet":"agentforce","desc":"AI agents assist with quote and order creation"}]
},

approvals: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Approvals",
  icon: "\u{2705}",
  color: "#0ea5e9",
  description: "The Approvals domain helps Revenue Cloud enforce business policies across quote and order transactions before they progress. Advanced Approvals uses Flow Orchestration to coordinate tailored, multi-step workflows with conditional logic and serial or parallel approval chains. A centralized Approvals app, audit visibility, previews, and notifications help submitters and reviewers track requests and act on assigned work.",
  components: [
    {id:"approval-workflows",name:"Approval Workflows",icon:"\u{1F504}",desc:"Approval Workflows define tailored, multi-step review processes for supported Revenue Cloud records, including quotes and orders. They use Flow Orchestration to coordinate approval steps, conditional logic, user input, and handoffs between teams. Workflows can arrange approvers in serial or parallel approval chains and maintain audit visibility throughout the process.",tags:["ApprovalProcess","ApprovalStep","ApprovalAction","ApprovalEscalation"],docs:["Approval workflows are Flow Approval Process flows that link tasks and assign them to users, groups, or queues. Designers can create an Autolaunched Flow Approval Process or a Record-Triggered Flow Approval Process in Flow Builder. Each workflow can contain stages, approval steps, background steps, Decision elements, and pauses for user input. The same limitations and best practices that apply to Flow Orchestration also apply to Advanced Approvals.","Serial approvals require approvers to complete their assigned tasks in sequence, with each step waiting for the preceding step to finish. Parallel approvals start multiple eligible steps at the same time and can use either First Response or All Must Approve behavior. Approval Chain Name identifies the group and sequence of approvers associated with a step or stage. Designers can also combine parallel chains with serial dependencies inside an individual chain.","Stage exit conditions must account for conditional steps that might never start. If a stage waits for every step to finish when some steps aren't triggered, the workflow can remain in a waiting state. Designers can instead use When the specified requirements are met, the stage is marked Complete and define logic for the applicable approval and rejection outcomes. After configuration, the flow must be saved and activated before it can process submissions."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.approvals_advanced_approvals.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Workflows are triggered when quotes or orders are submitted for approval"}]},
    {id:"approval-rules",name:"Approval Rules",icon:"\u{1F4CF}",desc:"Approval Rules determine which approval steps are required and when those steps begin or can be skipped. Revenue Cloud evaluates record values and configured conditions to route a submission through the applicable workflow paths. Smart Approvals and Rule-Based Auto-Approvals can eliminate repeated manual reviews for eligible resubmissions.",tags:["ApprovalRule","ApprovalCondition","ApprovalCriteria"],docs:["Approval step entry conditions control when work begins. A step can start when its stage starts, when another step is marked Complete, when specified requirements are met, or when a specified evaluation flow returns True. Decision elements and step conditions can apply transaction criteria, such as quote discount values, to determine which approval levels are required. Designers must also define completion and rejection outcomes so that the workflow advances correctly.","Smart Approvals reevaluates an approval step's existing entry conditions when a previously recalled or rejected record is resubmitted. If the values that satisfied the step in the previous submission still satisfy it, the step can be auto-approved instead of returning for manual review. Smart or Rule-Based Auto-Approvals apply only when the previous submission reached at least two steps and at least one of those steps was approved. A completely approved submission can't be recalled, so a later submission is treated as a new request and isn't eligible for auto-approval.","Rule-Based Auto-Approvals use custom logic, such as flows or Apex, to produce a boolean auto-approval decision. The Get Previous Related Record Details invocable action can retrieve data from a prior submission for comparison with the current record. A background step can run the custom autolaunched flow before the target approval step, and the target step uses the resulting boolean value for Auto-Approve Step. This approach supports evaluation logic that approval step entry conditions can't express, including cross-field comparisons."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Rules evaluate quote and order attributes to determine approval needs"},{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Pricing thresholds can trigger approval requirements"}]},
    {id:"approver-management",name:"Approver Management",icon:"\u{1F465}",desc:"Approver Management establishes the people, groups, and queues responsible for reviewing Revenue Cloud approval requests. Personas and permissions distinguish workflow design, administration, submission, and review responsibilities. Administrators can also manage access to related records and act on approval work items when necessary.",tags:["ApproverAssignment","ApprovalGroup","ApprovalAuthority","ApproverDelegate"],docs:["Advanced Approvals users require profiles and permissions appropriate to their responsibilities. An Approval Designer can design and activate approval workflows with permissions such as Manage Flow and Approval Designer. An approval admin can manage organization-wide settings, modify, recall, or cancel submissions, and review or override work items. Approval admins can use Override Approval Work Item to update a work item's status for any assignee.","Approval steps can be assigned to a specific user, group, or queue. When an approver needs access to a related record whose internal organization-wide sharing default is Private, an Apex trigger can create a share record for temporary access. After the approval request is complete, another trigger can delete the share record and revoke that access. For custom objects, an Apex sharing reason can restrict the shared access to the approval use case."],connections:[{"planet":"setup","desc":"Approver assignments are configured in user and permission settings"}]},
    {id:"approval-app",name:"Approval App",icon:"\u{1F4F1}",desc:"The Approval App provides a centralized experience for submitters, approvers, reviewers, and administrators to manage approval activity. Users can track submissions, review assigned work items, and approve or reject pending requests. Lightning components, previews, related lists, email, and Slack extend approval visibility and actions to relevant pages and channels.",tags:["ApprovalRequest","ApprovalDecision","ApprovalComment","ApprovalQueue"],docs:["The Approvals home page provides role-specific views of approval activity. Submitters can use Manage My Approval Submissions to see their requests and statuses, while approval administrators can use Manage All Pending Approval Submissions to view all submissions and act for other approvers. Approvers and reviewers can use Review My Approval Work Items to approve or reject pending records assigned to them. Access to the home page requires appropriate access to approval records, including Read access on ApprovalSubmission.","Approval pages can be customized with Lightning components, related lists, and custom actions. Components can show approval progress, outstanding approvals, activity, or a preview of the approval process. Related lists can expose work items, record history, files, and attachments. A record page can also include a custom action that invokes an autolaunched flow to submit a record, such as a quote, for approval.","Preview Approvals evaluates the current record's field values and simulates the steps, chains, dependencies, and assignees that would be triggered. The preview shows each step's Approval Chain, Assignee, Status, and Approval Condition without creating or modifying approval-related records. Approvers can also act from approval emails when email approval responses are enabled. Approvals in Slack supports approving or rejecting requests and adding comments in Slack, with those actions saved to Salesforce."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Approval decisions update quote and order status"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Configure approval users, permissions, and organization-wide Approval Settings.","Design and activate a Flow Approval Process for quotes and orders.","Evaluate transaction values to determine the applicable stages, chains, and approvers.","Submit the quote or order and create the approval request and assigned work items.","Route serial or parallel work items to users, groups, or queues for decisions.","Record approval actions and advance the transaction after the required authorizations are complete."],
  connections: [{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Quotes and orders go through approval workflows"}]
},

orchestrator: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Order Orchestration",
  icon: "\u{1F3AF}",
  color: "#3b82f6",
  description: "Order Orchestration coordinates the transition from submitted commercial orders, including contracted orders from Transaction Management, into executable fulfillment work in Revenue Cloud. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator decomposes commercial order items into technical fulfillment order line items and dynamically constructs fulfillment plans from scenarios, step groups, steps, scopes, and dependencies. It coordinates downstream systems, supports staged or plan-completion assetization, and reconciles in-flight amendments and cancellations while enforcing Point of No Return rules. The Decomposition Viewer and orchestration plan provide runtime visibility, while downstream billing is coordinated through billing triggers rather than Billing Events, which are associated with posted Invoice Line amounts.",
  components: [
    {id:"order-decomposition",name:"Order Decomposition",icon:"\u{1F9E9}",desc:"Order Decomposition translates commercial products in submitted orders into the technical products required by downstream fulfillment systems in Revenue Cloud. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator creates fulfillment order line items by applying decomposition rules, execution rules, decomposition scopes, and field and attribute mappings. It supports one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-one, and many-to-many decomposition relationships. The Decomposition Viewer shows the resulting product hierarchy, effective rules, mapped values, actions, and reasons for those actions.",tags:["OrderDecomposition","FulfillmentItem","DecompositionRule"],docs:["A decomposition rule associates a commercial product or product classification with a destination technical product. When an order is submitted, order line items are decomposed into fulfillment order line items according to qualifying rules. If multiple rules map to the same target, the rule with the highest priority takes precedence, with lower numeric values indicating higher priority. Execution rules can restrict a decomposition rule based on Sales Transaction Item context tags or product attributes.","Decomposition Scope controls how fulfillment order line items are instantiated and consolidated. Order Line Item creates an instance for each source order line item, while Order, Bundle, Account, and Custom scopes consolidate instances within their respective boundaries. The FOLI Quantity Calculation Method determines whether quantity is always one or aggregated from the source line items. When quantities use different units of measure, they must be converted to the technical product's unit of measure before order submission.","Field and Attribute Mapping copies or transforms commercial order data into the technical data required for fulfillment. As Is mapping copies the source value, List Mapping pairs source and destination values, and Expression Set Based Mapping performs more complex transformations. Mapping relies on context definitions, tags, and object-field mappings, and incompatible source and target data types can't be mapped. Fulfillment operators can use the Decomposition Viewer to inspect decomposition rules, field and attribute values, resulting actions, and Decision Explainer Service information when enrichment fails."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.dro_dynamic_revenue_orchestrator.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Decomposition processes contracted order line items"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Decomposed items determine billing trigger points"}]},
    {id:"fulfillment-plans",name:"Fulfillment Plans",icon:"\u{1F4CB}",desc:"Fulfillment Plans are dynamically constructed workflows that coordinate the steps required to fulfill an order in Revenue Cloud. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator selects fulfillment step definition groups through fulfillment scenarios and arranges the resulting fulfillment steps according to configured dependencies. Plans can coordinate work across internal teams, automated processes, and external systems. Runtime plan states, step states, dependencies, jeopardy indicators, and cross-plan dependencies provide visibility into fulfillment progress.",tags:["FulfillmentPlan","FulfillmentPlanStep","FulfillmentTemplate"],docs:["A fulfillment scenario determines whether a fulfillment step definition group is added to a fulfillment plan for a submitted order. Scenarios can apply to a product, a product classification, or the entire order, and can be restricted by actions and execution-rule conditions. If both product-classification and product-specific scenarios target the same step group, the product-specific scenario is applied. This lets designers reuse common fulfillment workstreams while supporting product-specific exceptions.","Fulfillment dependencies determine the sequence in which steps run. Dependency scopes include Plan, Bundle, Line Item, Custom, and Cross Plan, and designers must avoid dependency loops that can cause fulfillment to fail. Cross-plan dependencies connect steps in separate plans for related orders by using an Orchestration Group Key mapped through the Sales Transaction context definition. During execution, operators can inspect external dependencies and open related plans or steps.","A fulfillment plan progresses through Not Started, In Progress, Freezing, Frozen, and Completed states. The orchestration plan shows step status, dependencies, scheduled execution, jeopardy status, and SLA timing, and supports actions such as completing or retrying eligible steps. A plan must be Frozen before an in-flight amendment or cancellation can be applied. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator can also coordinate downstream actions such as provisioning, ERP updates, and billing triggers within the plan."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Plans are created from order line items after decomposition"}]},
    {id:"fulfillment-steps",name:"Fulfillment Steps",icon:"\u{1F4A1}",desc:"Fulfillment Steps are runtime instances of fulfillment step definitions in a Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator plan. Step definitions model manual work, automated flows, external callouts, milestones, pauses, staged assetization, and other fulfillment actions. Scope settings determine how many step instances are created for a plan, bundle, line item, or custom scope identifier. Execution rules, schedules, dependencies, run-as users, fallout rules, and SLA settings control how each step operates.",tags:["FulfillmentStep","StepAction","StepDependency","FulfillmentTask"],docs:["Auto Task steps invoke autolaunched Salesforce Flows, while Callout steps communicate synchronously or asynchronously with external systems through integration definitions. Manual Task steps assign work to a user or queue and can use Round Robin, Least Loaded, or Context Based assignment. Milestone steps mark significant points in the process after their dependencies complete, and Pause steps wait until a Resume On rule evaluates to true. Each step runs with the fulfillment user's permissions unless a different Run As User is specified.","Execution rules can evaluate Sales Transaction Item or Fulfillment Line Item context data to decide whether a step runs. When a rule isn't satisfied, Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator can skip only the step or, when Skip Branch is enabled, skip the dependent branch that relies solely on that step. Future-Dated Steps can schedule execution from a source line start date, a previous step's execution date, or a custom date mapped through a context definition. Designers can add positive or negative delays when supported by the selected schedule.","Fallout rules define how failed Auto Task and Callout steps are retried and where fatally failed steps are assigned. Retry policies can be Immediate, Monotonous, or Staggered, with configured retry counts and intervals. Jeopardy rules compare a step's Estimated Duration with its Jeopardy Threshold to identify work that is at risk of missing an SLA. Operators can monitor these states in the orchestration plan and retry or complete eligible failed steps individually or in bulk."],connections:[{"planet":"billing","desc":"Step completion can trigger billing events"}]},
    {id:"assetization",name:"Assetization",icon:"\u{1F4E6}",desc:"Assetization converts fulfilled commercial and technical order data into assets that represent the products and services delivered to a customer in Revenue Cloud. Order line items become Assets, while fulfillment order line items become Fulfillment Assets. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator can assetize eligible standalone products or bundles through a Staged Assetize step, or assetize fulfillment items when the fulfillment plan completes. Fulfillment Asset State Period records can preserve quantity and attribute configurations over distinct effective periods when time-awareness is enabled.",tags:["Asset","AssetAction","AssetStatePeriod","AssetRelationship"],docs:["A Staged Assetize step independently assetizes an eligible standalone order line item or an entire root product bundle before other long-running work completes. When applied to a standalone order line item, the step also assetizes its related fulfillment order line items. Applying the step to a non-root component in a bundle causes the step to be skipped. Ramp orders also skip staged assetization and assetize all segments when the fulfillment plan completes.","Fulfillment assets are related to commercial assets and inherit product and quantity information from their fulfillment order line items. The technical product's Fulfillment Quantity Calculation Method determines whether the fulfillment asset quantity remains one or reflects aggregated related asset quantities. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator reconciles fulfillment assets periodically based on related assets and asset state periods. The unit of measure on a fulfillment asset is inherited from its corresponding fulfillment order line item.","Time-awareness represents changing fulfillment asset configurations through Fulfillment Asset State Period records. Each period captures the quantity and attributes effective for a distinct segment of time, and newer periods can supersede earlier records. This model supports backdated and future-dated order changes without overwriting configurations outside the affected period. Time-aware Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator doesn't support staged assetization, and existing time-agnostic assets must be prepared before time-awareness is enabled."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Order lines are converted into customer assets"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Asset lifecycle events can trigger billing changes"}]},
    {id:"amendment-cancellation",name:"Amendment and Cancellation",icon:"\u{1F504}",desc:"Amendment and Cancellation manages changes to orders that are already undergoing fulfillment in Revenue Cloud. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator decomposes supplemental orders, calculates supplemental actions, and reconciles the existing fulfillment plan with the changed order. Completed work can be compensated or rolled back through configured Amend Step Groups and Cancel Step Groups. Point of No Return rules prevent unsupported changes after fulfillment reaches a defined stage unless an authorized override is used.",tags:["AmendmentAction","CancellationAction","AssetAmendment"],docs:["An in-flight order amendment can add line items, change quantities or attributes, or modify other eligible order data before fulfillment completes. An in-flight cancellation can remove individual items or cancel the order, subject to Point of No Return restrictions. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator compares the supplemental order with the original order and assigns supplemental actions such as Add, Amend, Cancel, or No Change to fulfillment lines. It then regenerates the affected portions of the fulfillment plan through Plan Reconciliation.","Amend Step Groups contain compensatory steps for completed work affected by an amendment. Cancel Step Groups contain rollback steps for work affected by a cancellation and can be configured to execute in reverse order. These groups are inserted after steps in terminal states and before pending work in the reconciled plan. Designers must also configure amend or cancel handling for compensatory steps if additional in-flight changes can occur.","A fulfillment step can be marked as a Point of No Return step. When that step moves to In Progress, the restriction propagates to related commercial and technical products according to their positions in the product bundle. Plans must reach the Frozen state before an in-flight change is submitted, while plans with active work remain in Freezing until the work finishes or is forcefully completed under configured rules. The PONR Override option can allow exceptional changes when the Submit Sales Transaction action is invoked with the applicable override parameter."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Amendment orders reference and modify existing assets"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Amendments trigger billing adjustments and cancellation charges"}]},
    {id:"fulfillment-workspace",name:"Fulfillment Workspace",icon:"\u{1F4BB}",desc:"Fulfillment Workspace provides the design-time canvas for building reusable orchestration workstreams in Revenue Cloud. Designers create fulfillment step definition groups, add step definitions, establish dependencies, and associate scenarios with the groups. Existing step groups can be imported by name or through associated fulfillment scenarios to promote reuse across workspaces. At runtime, the orchestration plan and Decomposition Viewer provide complementary visibility into plan execution and product decomposition.",tags:["FulfillmentWorkspace","FulfillmentDashboard","FulfillmentMonitor"],docs:["A fulfillment workspace is a graphical representation of fulfillment workstreams and their tasks. Fulfillment step definition groups represent logical workstreams such as procurement, shipping, installation, activation, or billing, while the steps within each group represent individual actions. Designers add step types, scopes, execution schedules, users, and in-flight change behavior directly from the workspace. Dependencies arrange the steps in the order in which they must execute.","Fulfillment step definition groups are reusable across workspaces. Designers can import groups from the Step Definition Group List or select Product Fulfillment Scenarios associated with the required groups. Imported groups retain their scenario associations where applicable. Workspace limits govern the number of groups, steps, and dependencies that appear on the canvas.","After order submission, fulfillment operators use the orchestration plan to monitor the plan as a whole and inspect individual steps. The plan supports searching, filtering by categories such as Manual Tasks, Fatally Failed, In Progress, Pending, or At Risk, and viewing step details and dependencies. The Decomposition Viewer separately exposes how commercial products became technical fulfillment products, including the applied rules and mapped values. Together, these interfaces help operators identify failed work, late steps, and decomposition issues."],connections:[{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Fulfillment metrics feed into operational dashboards"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Submit a contracted order to Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator.","Decompose commercial order items into fulfillment order line items.","Compose fulfillment plans from qualifying scenarios, step groups, and dependencies.","Execute manual, automated, scheduled, and external fulfillment steps.","Assetize eligible commercial and technical products and reconcile in-flight changes.","Coordinate downstream billing triggers and other fulfillment actions."],
  connections: [{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Contracted orders decomposed into fulfillment plans"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Fulfilled orders trigger billing events"}]
},

usage: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Usage and Consumption",
  icon: "\u{1F4CA}",
  color: "#f59e0b",
  description: "The Usage and Consumption domain defines consumption-based products and manages their usage through invoice-ready output in Revenue Cloud. Usage Management aggregates raw Transaction Journal entries into Usage Summary records, draws down grants held in usage entitlement buckets, identifies overages, and creates Liable Summary records for billing and analysis. Rate Management calculates usage-resource rates by using rate cards, rate card entries, and Rating Procedures. Billing, rather than Usage Management, creates billing schedules when an order is activated and uses metered usage to generate billing events and invoices.",
  components: [
    {id:"usage-modeling",name:"Usage Modeling",icon:"\u{1F4D0}",desc:"Usage Modeling defines the products, usage resources, units of measure, grants, and policies required for consumption-based offerings in Revenue Cloud. Sellable Usage Products provide usage-based services, and Anchor and Pack are the types of sellable products. Commitment products are modeled separately by using Monetary Commitment, Quantity Commitment, or Token Commitment usage model types. Rate cards and rating procedures define how usage resources are rated.",tags:["UsageModel","UsageMetric","UsageDefinition","UsageUnit"],docs:["Usage resources represent consumable entitlements or services, such as data storage, computing capacity, or API calls. Units of Measure quantify resource consumption, while Units of Measure Class records group related measurement types. Product Usage Resource associates a usage-based product with the resources included in its offering and can identify a token resource used to charge another usage resource.","Product Usage Grants define the grant or commitment associated with a Product Usage Resource. A grant can specify its quantity, validity period, drawdown order, Usage Grant Refresh Policy, and Usage Grant Rollover Policy. Product Usage Resource Policy records associate the Usage Aggregation Policy, Usage Overage Policy, Rating Frequency Policy, and Usage Commitment Policy that govern a resource when it is offered with a product.","Anchor Product is the main usage subscription and can include resource grants. Pack Product is an add-on that provides additional resource units for one-time use or at recurring intervals. Commitment products represent an agreement to consume a quantity, token amount, or monetary amount over a defined period in exchange for discounted rates. Rate Management uses rate cards, rate card entries, Rating Procedures, and Rating Discovery Procedures to calculate and retrieve the net unit rate for usage resources."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.um_usage_management_setup.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Usage models are linked to consumption-based products in the catalog"},{"planet":"rates","desc":"Usage metrics map to rate card entries for charge calculation"}]},
    {id:"consumption-tracking",name:"Consumption Tracking",icon:"\u{1F4C8}",desc:"Consumption Tracking records raw consumption and summarizes it for a defined usage entitlement and period in Revenue Cloud. Transaction Journal records store raw consumption logs for each usage resource. The Orchestrate Usage Management flow aggregates those entries into Usage Summary records and coordinates drawdown, rating, and liable-summary generation. Summary statuses track progress from initial creation through drawdown, rating, and completion of the Liable Summary.",tags:["UsageRecord","ConsumptionSummary","UsageIngestion","UsageAggregation"],docs:["The Generate Usage Summary flow aggregates Transaction Journal records according to the Usage Aggregation Policy. Supported accumulation methods include Sum and Peak, and the policy also specifies the usage accumulation period. The resulting Usage Summary stores the aggregated Transaction Journal entries for a usage entitlement and specified period.","Consumption data can be processed provisionally during an active billing period. The process creates zero-value Usage Summary, Usage Ratable Summary, and Liable Summary records in advance through the active billing period, then updates them as consumption arrives. After the configured waiting period ends, the Usage Summary is completed and no additional Transaction Journal entries are considered for that period.","Usage Ratable Summary records aggregate completed Usage Summary records and store the rate applied to overages. Rating can run provisionally during the billing cycle and is finalized after the billing period completes. Administrators can also generate an on-demand Usage Summary from Transaction Journal records or calculate an on-demand Usage Ratable Summary for unrated usage outside the scheduled process."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.um_consumption_management.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"rates","desc":"Consumption data is processed by rating procedures"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Aggregated usage generates billing events"}]},
    {id:"grant-management",name:"Grant Management",icon:"\u{1F381}",desc:"Grant Management tracks the usage entitlements provided or purchased with usage-based products in Revenue Cloud. Usage Entitlement Account records represent purchased product instances, while Usage Entitlement Bucket records act as wallets for individual usage resources. Usage Entitlement Entry records capture credits, debits, rollovers, and expired units for each tenure. Grant Binding determines whether resources are available to a single asset or pooled at a product, account, contract, or custom target.",tags:["UsageGrant","GrantBalance","GrantDepletion","GrantAllocation"],docs:["After a usage-based order is activated and assetized, Consumption Management creates the entitlement records used to manage resource balances. A parent Usage Entitlement Bucket represents the combined balance for a resource at the binding target, while child buckets represent grants from individual purchases, refreshes, rollovers, renewals, or amendments. The parent bucket aggregates the balances of its active child buckets.","Drawdown deducts consumption from available resource balances. Product Usage Grant determines the order in which eligible buckets are consumed by using Expiring First, Granted First, or Granted Last. Expiring First processes the bucket with the expiration date closest to the current date, while the other options prioritize the oldest or newest grant.","Usage Grant Refresh Policy determines whether grants refresh and at what frequency. Usage Grant Rollover Policy determines whether unused grants expire or roll over after their initial validity period. Grant Binding can scope a resource pool to Self or to a target such as Product, Account, Contract, or a custom object, and the binding target can't be changed after the order is activated and assetized."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.um_wallet_management.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"billing","desc":"Grant purchases appear as line items on invoices"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Grants are established when usage-based orders are activated"}]},
    {id:"overage-handling",name:"Overage Handling",icon:"\u{26A0}",desc:"Overage Handling identifies resource consumption that exceeds the available grant or commitment in Revenue Cloud. After drawdown, the excess quantity is recorded on usage summaries and aggregated into Usage Ratable Summary records. Rate Management applies the applicable rates to determine the charge for the excess consumption. Liable Summary records store the calculated overage quantity and amount for downstream invoicing.",tags:["OveragePolicy","OverageCharge","UsageAlert","UsageThreshold"],docs:["The Process Consumption Overages batch job processes aggregated Usage Summary records and performs drawdown against available grants. When consumption exceeds the available Usage Entitlement Bucket balance, the remaining quantity is treated as overage. The applicable Usage Overage Policy determines whether those overage units are chargeable.","Rate Management calculates overage charges by using rate cards and Rating Procedures. A Rating Frequency Policy determines when rating is initiated for ratable summary records, and Rating Discovery Procedures retrieve the applicable rate cards, rate card entries, and adjustments. Usage Ratable Summary records store the aggregated usage summaries and the calculated net unit rate applied to the overage.","The Generate Liable Summary process combines rated usage information into invoice-ready Liable Summary records. During processing, a Liable Summary can contain provisional overage quantity and amount for the billing period to date; after completion, it becomes ready for invoicing. Billing selects ready usage billing schedules and Liable Summary records to generate invoice lines, and posted invoices update the Liable Summary status to Invoiced."],connections:[{"planet":"rates","desc":"Overage rates are defined on rate cards"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Overage charges generate distinct billing line items"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Define usage-based products, resources, units of measure, grants, policies, rate cards, and rating procedures.","Activate the order to create the asset and usage entitlements, and let Billing create the related usage billing schedules.","Record raw consumption and aggregate Transaction Journal entries into Usage Summary records.","Draw down available grants, identify overages, and rate usage resources by using rate cards and Rating Procedures.","Generate Liable Summary records and send metered usage to Billing to generate billing events and invoices."],
  connections: [{"planet":"rates","desc":"Usage resources rated using rate cards"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Metered usage generates billing events"}]
},

billing: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Billing and Invoicing",
  icon: "\u{1F4B3}",
  color: "#f97316",
  description: "Billing and Invoicing in Revenue Cloud converts activated orders and invoice-ready usage into Billing Schedules, invoices, Credit Memos, Debit Memos, payments, and related accounting records. When an order is activated, Revenue Cloud creates a Billing Schedule for each order item; usage invoicing is driven by usage schedules and Liable Summaries, while Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator breaks commercial orders into technical fulfillment tasks rather than supplying billing events. Billing Policies, Billing Treatments, payment terms, Billing Profiles, Legal Entities, Tax Engines, and related settings govern schedule creation, invoice generation, taxation, and settlement. Agentforce assists with billing inquiries, and Revenue Cloud intelligence dashboards provide billing and revenue analytics.",
  components: [
    {id:"billing-schedules",name:"Billing Schedules",icon:"\u{1F4C5}",desc:"Billing Schedules store the order item information used in invoicing and define when and how each order product is billed. When an order is activated, Revenue Cloud creates a billing schedule for each order item and groups related schedules in Billing Schedule Groups. For usage-based products, order activation creates a billing schedule and billing schedule group for each binding instance and usage resource pair. BillingContext maps billing transactions with BillingScheduleGroups and Orders.",tags:["BillingSchedule","BillingScheduleGroup","BillingPeriod"],docs:["Billing schedules are generated from activated orders by using the Order to Billing Schedule flow, Create Billing Schedules for Orders API, or Create Standalone Billing Schedules API. Revenue Cloud recommends using only one active flow to generate schedules because multiple active flows can create duplicate schedules for the same order. Billing schedules can also be created directly from external transactions or other Salesforce objects by using the Create Standalone Billing Schedules API.","Billing schedules for original, amended, renewed, and canceled assets are maintained within the applicable Billing Schedule Group. Amendment and cancellation schedules reference the original billing schedule, and cancellation dates are populated across schedules related to the asset. Billing Schedule Groups provide a consolidated view of schedules generated from one asset, including schedules for new orders and amendment orders.","The generated schedule inherits billing information such as quantity, unit of measure, start and end dates, payment terms, tax treatment, and legal entity from the applicable transaction and configuration. Billing prioritizes dates on Order Item Detail or Quote Line Detail records before using dates from the parent item. Billing admins can update supported Billing Schedule Group values, including the bill day of month, next billing date override, billing start month, addresses, tax treatment, and payment terms.","Usage billing schedules remain Ready for Invoicing and stay open beyond their end date to accommodate late usage and additional orders for the same binding instance. Revenue Cloud uses liable summary dates instead of calculating invoice periods for these schedules. The liable summary, usage entitlement bucket, and usage entitlement account must share the grant binding target before the usage schedule can be invoiced."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.billing_schedules_and_schedule_groups.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Billing schedules are created from activated order line items"},{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Fulfillment completion can trigger billing schedule activation"}]},
    {id:"invoice-generation",name:"Invoice Generation",icon:"\u{1F4C4}",desc:"Invoice Generation converts eligible billing schedules and related billing data into draft or posted invoices and invoice lines. Revenue Cloud supports scheduled invoice batch runs, on-demand invoice generation for accounts and orders, and standalone invoice ingestion. Invoice grouping settings determine whether schedules produce consolidated or separate invoices. Generated invoices can then be posted, voided, documented, emailed, and settled.",tags:["Invoice","InvoiceLine","InvoiceBatch","InvoiceGeneration"],docs:["Billing Batch Schedulers automate invoice generation by selecting eligible billing schedules according to criteria such as target date, billing batch, charge type, legal entity, account, and currency. Invoice batch runs process qualifying billing schedules in parallel and can create either draft or posted invoices. Billing calculates estimated tax for draft invoices and actual tax for posted invoices through the configured tax engine.","Users can generate pending invoices on demand from Account or Order records. Revenue Cloud selects related billing schedules whose status is Ready for Invoicing and whose next billing date is on or before the selected target date. The Invoice Creation API and corresponding flow actions support the same type of programmatic generation for accounts, orders, or lists of billing schedules.","Invoice grouping is configured on each billing schedule. The Default type consolidates schedules that share the default grouping fields, the Billing Schedule type generates a separate invoice, and the Custom type combines the default grouping with a custom invoice group key. The default group includes the billing account, bill-to contact, currency ISO code, payment term, tax engine, legal entity, and saved payment method.","The Invoice Ingestion API creates standalone invoices or imports invoice data from internal or external transactions. It can create Invoice, Invoice Line, Invoice Line Tax, and Invoice Address Group records from the supplied data. Posted invoices can be voided, while failed runs and billing schedules in Processing, Void In Progress, or Error status can be handled with the documented recovery APIs."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.billing_invoice_generation.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Invoice data feeds revenue dashboards and analytics"}]},
    {id:"billing-policies",name:"Billing Policies",icon:"\u{1F4DC}",desc:"Billing Policies group Billing Treatments that define how Revenue Cloud invoices order items. A billing policy is related to products and is automatically applied to order items when an order is created. When the order is activated, a Billing Treatment is assigned according to the policy's treatment selection. Revenue Cloud uses the selected treatment and its treatment item to create billing schedules.",tags:["BillingPolicy","BillingPolicyRule","ProrationPolicy","DunningPolicy"],docs:["Billing Treatments define whether an order item is billed and how its total amount is represented in billing schedules. The Exclude From Billing value can prevent Revenue Cloud from creating billing schedules, Billing Schedule Groups, and invoices for selected order items. A Billing Treatment Item specifies billing behavior such as Advance, Arrears, or milestone-aligned billing and defines how the order item's amount is distributed through its lifecycle.","Billing Treatment Selection can be Default, Manual, or Legal Entity. Default uses the active default treatment on the Billing Policy, Manual uses the treatment specified on the order product, and Legal Entity attempts to match a treatment to the applicable legal entity before falling back to the default. If a product has no Billing Policy, Revenue Cloud uses the default Billing Treatment configured for the Salesforce org.","Billing Policies and their related records must be created and activated in dependency order. Administrators create a draft Billing Policy, its Billing Treatments, and the related Billing Treatment Items, then activate the items, treatments, and policy. Only active policies and treatments participate in treatment resolution and billing schedule creation."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.billing_policies_and_treatments.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"setup","desc":"Billing policies are configured in revenue settings"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Policies determine how amendments affect billing"}]},
    {id:"payment-processing",name:"Payment Processing",icon:"\u{1F4B5}",desc:"Payment Processing manages the collection and application of customer payments for posted invoices and supports refunds when required. Revenue Cloud connects native or third-party payment gateways through Salesforce Payments and payment gateway adapters. Payment Schedules and Payment Schedule Items define the amounts, methods, dates, and accounts used in payment processing. Payment batch runs process eligible schedule items and apply resulting payments to invoices or invoice lines.",tags:["Payment","PaymentMethod","PaymentAllocation","PaymentGateway"],docs:["A Payment Schedule contains one or more Payment Schedule Items, with each item representing a payment to process. Revenue Cloud can automatically create these records after an invoice is posted by using the configured Payment Schedule Policy, Payment Schedule Treatment, treatment detail, and distribution method. Payment admins and operations users can also create schedules and schedule items manually.","Payment batch runs select Payment Schedule Items that are Ready for Processing and belong to open Payment Schedules. Successful processing creates Payment records and applies them according to the configured Credit and Payment Application Level. Application can occur at either the Invoice level or Invoice Line level, and Revenue Cloud updates balances, settlement status, processed amounts, and related application records.","Salesforce Payments supports native payment gateways, while payment gateway adapters connect Revenue Cloud to external gateways. Saved Payment Methods let customers reuse securely tokenized payment details rather than entering them for every transaction. Payment retry rule sets and rules can retry failed payments at fixed or staggered intervals and can use an alternate payment method on the final attempt.","Revenue Cloud can issue refunds when settled subscriptions are amended or canceled. The process creates and applies credits, calculates the refundable amount, and initiates the refund against the original payment method when the payment is associated with a gateway. Payments, refunds, and their related financial records can also participate in transaction journal creation and multi-currency accounting."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.billing_payments.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Payment data feeds into revenue collection dashboards"}]},
    {id:"tax-engine",name:"Tax Engine",icon:"\u{1F4B0}",desc:"The Tax Engine calculates estimated tax for draft invoices and actual tax for posted invoices. Revenue Cloud supports the Revenue Standard Tax Engine, custom tax engines implemented with TaxEngineAdapter, and partner tax applications. Tax Policies and Tax Treatments determine whether products are taxable, which engine is used, and which tax codes apply. Calculated results are stored in tax lines related to invoice lines.",tags:["TaxEngine","TaxCalculation","TaxRate","TaxExemption"],docs:["A Tax Engine Provider identifies the Apex adapter used to communicate with a tax engine, while a Tax Engine stores the provider instance, named credential, seller details, address, and status. Active Tax Engines can be referenced by Tax Treatments. Revenue Cloud can also import externally calculated Invoice Line Tax records for draft invoices when tax is calculated outside the product.","Tax Policies are related to order products and use Default, Manual, or Legal Entity treatment selection. A Tax Treatment specifies whether the transaction is taxable, the Tax Engine, tax code, Tax Policy, and optionally a legal entity. Tax Treatment Items provide product-specific tax codes when Use Tax Treatment Items is selected.","The Revenue Standard Tax Engine calculates taxes internally from configured Tax Rate records and the Revenue Standard Tax Entries decision table. It matches transaction values such as country, state, currency ISO code, product code, and legal entity, then applies matching rates in priority order. Tax rates can be percentages or flat amounts and can use gross or net application logic.","Invoice Line Tax records store details such as Tax Amount, Tax Code, Tax Effective Date, Tax Name, Tax Rate, and external transaction references. Revenue Cloud aggregates line tax amounts into invoice totals. When header-level tax capture is configured, the consolidated header tax amount drives invoice and credit memo balances while line-level tax details can remain available for reporting or reconciliation."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.billing_tax_configuration.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"setup","desc":"Tax rules and rates are configured in revenue settings"}]},
    {id:"credit-debit-notes",name:"Credit and Debit Notes",icon:"\u{1F4DD}",desc:"Credit and Debit Notes adjust amounts owed after billing transactions have been created. A Credit Memo reduces the amount a buyer owes under an earlier invoice, while a Debit Memo adds an additional charge. Revenue Cloud supports standalone credit memos, credits applied to invoices or invoice lines, and debit memo lines converted into invoice lines. Tax, legal entity, accounting period, and product details can be retained on the related lines.",tags:["CreditNote","DebitNote","CreditNoteLine","FinancialAdjustment"],docs:["Credit memos can address billing errors, returns, discounts, cancellations, or overpayments. Users can create standalone credit memos or create and apply credits directly from selected invoice lines. Credit application follows the configured application level and reduces the balance of an Invoice or Invoice Line while creating the corresponding application record.","Revenue Cloud can automatically convert negative invoice lines into posted Credit Memo Lines after an invoice is posted. It can then apply the resulting credits to settle the originating invoice or its lines. Unapplied posted credit memos can be voided, and pending or errored credit memos can be recovered.","Debit Memos capture additional amounts for undercharges, cancellation or suspension fees, late payment charges, and similar adjustments. Each Debit Memo Line identifies the product or reference record, charge amount, tax treatment, legal entity, dates, and addresses for the additional charge. Posted debit memo lines that are ready for invoice generation are converted into Invoice Lines during invoice processing.","Revenue Cloud can configure gapless sequential numbering for posted invoices and Credit Memos through Sequence Policies. A sequence pattern combines static text with dynamic placeholders, including the required {SequenceValue}. Sequence Gap Reconciliation records track missing values when gapless numbering is enforced."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.billing_credit_memos.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Amendments and cancellations can generate credit notes"},{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Credit and debit note trends appear in revenue analytics"}]},
    {id:"billing-subscriptions",name:"Billing for Subscriptions",icon:"\u{1F504}",desc:"Billing for Subscriptions supports recurring, ramped, milestone-based, and usage-based billing models. Product Selling Models and billing configuration determine subscription terms, billing frequency, billing type, period boundaries, and proration. Revenue Cloud creates schedules when orders are activated and updates them for amendments, renewals, cancellations, swaps, and supported end-date changes. Usage invoicing is driven by usage schedules and liable summaries.",tags:["SubscriptionBilling","SubscriptionSchedule","SubscriptionRenewal","CoTermination"],docs:["Term-defined and evergreen products can use billing frequencies that differ from their pricing frequencies when the supported configuration is enabled. Period Boundary settings such as Anniversary, Align to Calendar, Day of Period, and Last Day of Period determine how billing periods align to the subscription. Advance billing invoices an upcoming period, while Arrears billing invoices a period after it has occurred.","Proration adjusts charges when a subscription starts, changes, or ends partway through a billing period. Revenue Cloud applies the configured proration policy and remainder strategy to partial periods. Amendments, renewals, cancellations, price changes, and subscription end-date changes generate or update billing schedules so that later invoices reflect the asset lifecycle.","For usage-based products, order activation creates schedules for each binding instance and usage resource pair. Usage Management aggregates consumption, processes drawdowns, retrieves rates, and generates liable summaries containing invoice-ready overages. Revenue Cloud invoices a usage schedule only when the Liable Summary is Ready for Invoicing and the required grant binding and usage resource relationships match.","Ramp deals create separate billing schedules for ramp segments while retaining a Billing Schedule Group for the asset. Milestone billing links a Billing Milestone Plan to a schedule and generates invoice lines when date-based or event-based milestones become ready for invoicing. These models let Revenue Cloud align charges with subscription periods, consumption, or project progress."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Subscription billing aligns with order and amendment lifecycle"},{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Subscription activation triggers billing schedule creation"}]},
    {id:"invoice-templates",name:"Invoice Templates",icon:"\u{1F4C3}",desc:"Invoice Templates define the content and layout of generated invoice and invoice preview PDF documents. Revenue Cloud provides Default Invoice Template and Default Invoice Preview Template for immediate use after Document Generation is enabled. Administrators can clone and customize these templates or create document templates that meet business and branding requirements. Billing Profiles can select account-specific invoice templates.",tags:["InvoiceTemplate","TemplateSection","TemplateLayout"],docs:["After Document Generation is enabled, the Default Invoice Template is used for invoice PDFs unless another template resolves at a more specific level. The template can include invoice dates, numbers, due dates, totals, balances, account addresses, product details, quantities, unit prices, tax amounts, and billing periods. Bundled invoice lines can be displayed hierarchically in generated documents.","For on-demand invoice generation, Revenue Cloud first checks the invoice's Billing Profile for an Invoice Template. If none is specified, it checks the default Billing Profile for the billing account, then falls back to the org-level default invoice document template. This resolution supports account-specific documents without changing the global template.","The Default Invoice Preview Template generates PDF documents for invoice previews. Custom preview templates should use the data tokens provided by the default preview template to avoid missing data. Users can generate preview PDFs for supported Account and Order previews and share them before actual invoices are produced.","Generated Invoice Documents are stored with the related invoice and are also accessible through Files. Invoice documents can include a company logo when the supported Document Generation configuration and file naming requirements are met. Posted invoice documents can be attached to customer emails according to the configured email delivery preferences."],connections:[]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Configure Billing Policies, Billing Treatments, Tax Engines, payment terms, Legal Entities, and Billing Profiles in Revenue Cloud settings.","Activate orders to create Billing Schedules and Billing Schedule Groups while Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator manages technical fulfillment tasks separately.","Aggregate consumption and prepare Liable Summaries for invoice-ready usage charges.","Generate draft or posted invoices from eligible schedules, then calculate taxes and produce invoice documents.","Apply Credit Memos, Debit Memos, payments, and refunds, and use Agentforce to assist with billing inquiries.","Review billing and revenue performance in Revenue Cloud intelligence dashboards."],
  connections: [{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Orders trigger billing schedule creation"},{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Fulfilled orders trigger billing events"},{"planet":"usage","desc":"Metered usage generates billing events"},{"planet":"setup","desc":"Billing policies and tax engines in settings"},{"planet":"agentforce","desc":"AI agents help with billing inquiries"},{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Dashboards display billing and revenue data"}]
},

procedures: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Business Rules Engine",
  icon: "\u{2699}",
  color: "#8b5cf6",
  description: "The Business Rules Engine domain provides reusable decision, lookup, and calculation logic for Revenue Cloud. Procedures and expression sets arrange executable elements for pricing, rating, qualification, discovery, and related processes, while decision tables supply rule-based lookup outcomes. Context definitions provide the logical data model and mappings used to exchange data between applications and procedures, and batch jobs support monitored processing of larger workloads. Transaction Management processes quote line imports through the Import Quote Line Items flow and a Data Processing Engine definition that reads CSV data and creates quote line items.",
  components: [
    {id:"procedure-definitions",name:"Procedure Definitions",icon:"\u{1F4CB}",desc:"Procedure Definitions organize executable logic for Revenue Cloud processes such as pricing, rating, qualification, and pricing discovery. A procedure is built as an ordered set of specialized elements that can call decision tables, perform calculations, and map values through context tags. Designers simulate procedure versions before activation and can use procedure plan definitions to sequence procedures within a broader transaction process.",tags:["OmniProcess","ProcedureDefinition","ProcedureStep","ProcedureVersion"],docs:["Revenue Cloud uses several documented procedure types for specific business processes. Pricing procedures calculate product prices through ordered pricing elements, rating procedures calculate the final net rate of a usage resource, and qualification rule procedures evaluate product or category eligibility. Discovery procedures retrieve contributing data used by pricing or rating. Procedure plan definitions can combine procedures into sections and organize their execution for a supported process.","Create a procedure with the appropriate usage type and associate it with a compatible context definition. Add the required elements, map their variables to context tags, set version rank and effective dates, and select which element results are included in the output. Simulate the procedure with sample values to verify the result, then activate the validated version. Rules Engine Designer and runtime permissions control access to design and execute applicable Business Rules Engine logic."],connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Pricing procedures use the procedure engine for calculations"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Procedures handle document processing and transformations"}]},
    {id:"expression-sets",name:"Expression Sets",icon:"\u{1F9EE}",desc:"Expression Sets provide the ordered logic used to build configurable procedures in Revenue Cloud. Their elements can perform lookups, assignments, formulas, list operations, aggregation, rounding, and other documented calculations. Expression Set Builder supports simulation and activation so designers can validate a version before using it at run time.",tags:["ExpressionSet","Expression","ExpressionVariable"],docs:["Revenue Cloud provides expression set templates that can be cloned into executable procedures. For example, the Product Discovery Pricing Procedure template can be saved as a pricing procedure, while predefined rating procedure templates can be cloned for Rate Management. Qualification Procedure Elements such as EvaluateQualification and EvaluateDisqualification are building blocks of expression sets that look up decision tables containing qualification rules.","Configure each expression set version by adding elements in the required order and mapping input and output variables to context tags or supported constant resources. Set a rank and an effective start date, select Include in Output where required, and save the version before simulation. Simulation accepts simplified field input or JSON input and shows the resulting output; pricing and rating simulations can also provide waterfall details. Activate the version only after its calculations and mappings produce the expected result."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.business_rules_engine.htm&language=en_US",connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Expression sets perform pricing calculations"},{"planet":"rates","desc":"Rating procedures use expressions for charge calculation"}]},
    {id:"decision-tables",name:"Decision Tables",icon:"\u{1F5C2}",desc:"Decision Tables are advanced lookup tables that evaluate input values against business rules and return matching field values as outcomes. They can read rules from standard objects, custom objects, or custom metadata types and support operators on input fields. Revenue Cloud uses decision tables in qualification, pricing, rating, tax, and orchestration-related processes.",tags:["DecisionTable","DecisionTableRow","DecisionTableColumn"],docs:["A decision table selects fields from a source object as conditions and result fields as outputs. Operators define how each input value is evaluated, while source filters can narrow the source records included in the table. Advanced decision tables can provide multiple matching outcomes for the same input set, although individual Revenue Cloud features can impose more specific resolution behavior. For product qualification, separate decision tables can evaluate qualification and disqualification records for products or categories.","Create the table with the documented application usage type, source object, conditions, results, and matching behavior required by the consuming procedure. Activate the decision table before referencing it from a procedure element, and deactivate it before changing its configuration. Refresh object-backed decision tables after their source pricing, rating, contract, tax, or qualification data changes. Users require the appropriate Rules Engine Designer or Rules Engine Runtime access to design or execute decision tables."],connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Decision tables drive matrix-based pricing lookups"}]},
    {id:"context-definitions",name:"Context Definitions",icon:"\u{1F4DA}",desc:"Context Definitions provide the logical data model that connects Revenue Cloud applications, procedures, and underlying data sources. A definition contains nodes, attributes, context tags, and mappings that describe relationships between logical entities and Salesforce objects. Context Service hydrates mapped data for consuming applications and can persist procedure results through the configured mappings. Revenue Cloud includes predefined context definitions that can be extended or cloned for supported use cases.",tags:["ContextDefinition","ContextNode","ContextMapping"],docs:["Context Service forms a data abstraction layer between applications and procedures. Nodes represent logical entities, attributes represent logical fields, and context tags uniquely identify nodes or attributes within a context definition. Context mapping connects this logical structure to standard objects, custom objects, and other supported data sources. Revenue Cloud provides predefined definitions such as SalesTransactionContext, ProductDiscoveryContext, RateManagementContext, RatingDiscoveryContext, BillingContext, and StandaloneBillingContext.","Extend a standard context definition when you want to preserve its nodes, attributes, and mappings while adding custom components. Extended definitions inherit standard metadata and are automatically upgraded when the source definition changes, although an administrator may need to use Sync Now if automatic synchronization fails. Cloned definitions can be changed independently but aren't automatically upgraded from their standard source. Activate the definition and use compatible mappings before associating it with pricing, rating, qualification, billing, or orchestration procedures."],docUrl:"https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=ind.context_service.htm&language=en_US",connections:[]},
    {id:"batch-processing",name:"Batch Processing",icon:"\u{1F504}",desc:"Batch Processing supports asynchronous work across groups of records and provides job status information for operational monitoring. Revenue Cloud uses batch jobs and Data Processing Engine definitions for documented processes such as data transformation, usage summaries, invoice operations, and large-volume migration. Transaction Management handles quote line imports through the Import Quote Line Items flow and a Data Processing Engine definition that processes CSV data and creates quote line items.",tags:["BatchProcess","BatchJob","BatchSchedule","BatchMonitor"],docs:["Data Processing Engine definitions transform Salesforce data and can be invoked from flows or scheduled processes. Administrators can monitor Data Processing Engine and other batch activity through workflow monitoring and batch job records. For large datasets, Salesforce recommends dividing data into batches, using chunked processing, planning for record-level failures, and implementing retry logic. Setting allOrNone to false for data manipulation operations allows partial batch success during large migrations.","For quote line imports, turn on Import Quote Line Items, configure the flow that opens when a user clicks Import Lines, and create a Data Processing Engine definition from the appropriate template. The definition processes CSV rows, creates quote line items, and can also create related bundle lines, relationships, and attributes through its configured nodes. Users can import multiple files within the documented quote limits and review Revenue Transaction Error Log records when processing fails. This quote-line import implementation belongs to Transaction Management rather than to a Document processing engine."],connections:[{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Batch job status and metrics appear in the operations console"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Enable Context Service and assign the required design-time and runtime permissions.","Extend or configure a context definition, then map its nodes and attributes to the required data sources.","Create and activate decision tables that expose the conditions and outcomes required by the logic.","Assemble the procedure or expression set, map context tags, simulate the version, and activate it.","Invoke the active logic from the applicable Revenue Cloud pricing, rating, qualification, or transaction process.","Monitor batch jobs, and process quote line CSV imports through Transaction Management's Import Quote Line Items flow and Data Processing Engine definition."],
  connections: [{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Document processing engine handles quote line imports"}]
},

agentforce: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Agentforce for Revenue",
  icon: "\u{1F916}",
  color: "#a855f7",
  description: "Agentforce for Revenue brings AI-powered assistance to Revenue Cloud product-to-cash operations through Agentforce Employee Agent templates. Revenue Quote Management automates product selection, quote creation, and quote management, helping sales reps create and update initial, renewal, and amendment quotes and their line items; it doesn’t provide order creation or update actions. Billing Employee Assistance and Billing Service Assistance provide conversational access to invoice explanations, balances, payment details, invoice documents, payment plans, due dates, and collections guidance. Agents use generative AI for reasoning and orchestration while operating with the applicable Revenue Cloud setup, licenses, permissions, authenticated access, and Experience Cloud configuration.",
  components: [
    {id:"ai-agent-setup",name:"AI Agent Setup",icon:"\u{2699}",desc:"AI Agent Setup configures Agentforce Employee Agent templates for Revenue Cloud product-to-cash use cases. Available templates include Revenue Quote Management, Billing Employee Assistance, and Billing Service Assistance, with each experience requiring the applicable Revenue Cloud features, licenses, permission sets, and user access. Administrators can also expose Revenue Quote Management on a partner site or Billing Service Assistance on a community site by configuring Experience Cloud, Embedded Messaging, Omni-Channel routing, and authenticated users.",tags:["AgentDefinition","AgentSkill","AgentGuardrail","AgentPersona"],docs:["Revenue Cloud agent templates are Agentforce Employee Agent templates. Before creating an agent, set up Einstein Generative AI, turn on Agentforce, and enable the Revenue Cloud solutions required by the selected template. Revenue Quote Management requires the Revenue Cloud Advanced license and the Agentforce Employee Agent add-on, along with permissions for product discovery, pricing, tax calculation, quote lifecycle operations, and product configuration. Billing templates require access to the applicable Billing data and permissions, and Billing Employee Assistance also requires Prompt Template User and flow permissions.","Create an agent from the Revenue Quote Management, Billing Employee Assistance, or Billing Service Assistance template. Review the included topics and actions, add or customize supported topics and actions when needed, and test any customized behavior before deployment. Create a custom permission set to grant users access to the agent, optionally turn on audit trails to track Agentforce action usage, and activate the agent after validation. Revenue Quote Management setup can also include indexed product data, semantic search, multiple-currency flow changes, and Rate Management and Usage Management when the Consumption Management topic is used.","For external access, prepare Customer Community or Partner Community users and assign the custom profiles, Revenue Cloud permissions, and Agentforce permission set required for the experience. Configure Embedded Messaging, enable Omni-Channel with skills-based and direct-to-agent routing, and create a fallback queue for unresolved conversations. An Omni-Channel flow routes work to the selected agent and sends unresolved requests to the fallback queue. Add the Embedded Messaging component to the Experience Cloud site, use credential-based user verification, and publish the site."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Agents are configured with skills for quote and order operations"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Agents are configured with skills for billing inquiries"}]},
    {id:"agent-actions",name:"Agent Actions",icon:"\u{26A1}",desc:"Agent Actions connect Revenue Cloud agent topics to product selection, quote management, consumption management, invoice explanation, collections, and billing inquiry operations. Revenue Quote Management actions help sales reps find products and create or update initial, renewal, and amendment quotes and quote line items; the template doesn’t provide actions for creating or updating orders. Billing actions retrieve account balances, payment details, invoice documents, invoice line explanations, payment plans, due dates, and collections guidance from authorized Revenue Cloud data.",tags:["AgentAction","ActionDefinition","ActionConfirmation","ActionComposition"],docs:["The Product Selection topic uses Find Products, Get Product Details, and Get Rule-Based Product Recommendations (Beta). It lets sales users search the product catalog with natural-language requests, refine results by customer criteria, review product attributes, and receive recommendations driven by configuration rules. Indexed product data and semantic search can improve matching, although semantic search can be turned off when it doesn’t return the expected results. Product answers are based on product catalog data and don’t provide legal, commercial, or policy advice.","The Quote Management topic provides actions including Add Quote Line Item to Quote, Apply Discount To Quote Line Item, Create Initial Quote, Create Renewal Quote, Create Amendment Quote, Query Quote Line Items (Beta), Update Quote Details, and Update Quote Line Item Details. It also uses supporting actions to identify records, retrieve product selling models, query records, summarize records, and update records. These actions help authenticated users create and update quotes and line items, apply discounts, modify quote status, and synchronize or unsynchronize a quote with its opportunity. Revenue Quote Management is scoped to product selection and quote management; order creation or order updates aren’t actions of this template, although Revenue Cloud transaction processes can subsequently create orders from accepted quotes.","Billing Employee Assistance uses Invoice Line Explanation, Billing Collections Management, and Billing Inquiries topics. Its actions can explain invoice lines, summarize billing health, recommend a dunning strategy, retrieve an account balance or next due payment, generate payment plan details, and return an invoice document PDF. Billing Service Assistance provides customers with conversational invoice-charge explanations through an authenticated Experience Cloud billing portal. All responses operate on Revenue Cloud data available to the user through the configured permissions and site access."],connections:[{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Actions can create and modify quotes and orders"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Actions can look up and explain billing details"},{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Actions can generate reports and analytics summaries"}]},
    {id:"agent-monitoring",name:"Agent Monitoring",icon:"\u{1F4CA}",desc:"Agent Monitoring covers validation and oversight of Revenue Cloud agents before and after activation. Administrators test customized topics and actions, can turn on audit trails to track Agentforce action usage, and use access controls to keep agent operations within authorized Revenue Cloud data. Monitoring should distinguish agent activity from downstream Revenue Cloud processing, especially because Revenue Quote Management creates and updates quotes and line items rather than orders.",tags:["AgentMonitoring","AgentMetrics","ConversationLog","AgentAlert"],docs:["Test an agent after customizing its topics or actions and before activation. Validate representative product searches, quote creation and updates, invoice explanations, balance inquiries, payment details, and collections requests according to the topics included in the selected template. For external experiences, also verify Embedded Messaging, authenticated access, Omni-Channel routing, and fallback queue behavior. Activate the agent only after the configured topics, actions, permissions, and routing behave as expected.","Turn on audit trails when the organization needs to track the usage of Agentforce actions. Audit data supports oversight of which configured actions the agent invokes, while Salesforce Trust Layer protections and user-level permissions restrict access to authorized pricing and customer data. Review agent access whenever licenses, permission sets, Experience Cloud profiles, or Revenue Cloud feature configuration changes. Revenue Cloud agents use the platform data model and APIs rather than a separate integration layer."],connections:[{"planet":"intelligence","desc":"Agent performance metrics feed into operational dashboards"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Configure the required Revenue Cloud features, licenses, permissions, and authenticated user access.","Create an agent from the appropriate Agentforce Employee Agent template and review its topics and actions.","Accept a request for Product Selection, Quote Management, consumption details, or a billing inquiry.","Invoke authorized actions to find products, create or update quotes and quote line items, or retrieve billing details.","Continue accepted quote transactions through Revenue Cloud processes for order creation where applicable.","Test the response and routing behavior, activate the agent, and track action usage with audit trails when enabled."],
  connections: [{"planet":"transactions","desc":"AI agents assist with quote and order creation"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"AI agents help with billing inquiries"}]
},

intelligence: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Revenue Intelligence",
  icon: "\u{1F4CA}",
  color: "#ec4899",
  description: "This graph domain groups the Revenue Management Intelligence analysis capability with the separately documented Revenue Cloud Operations Console. Revenue Management Intelligence uses Tableau Einstein dashboards and Data 360 to analyze billing, pricing, order fulfillment, subscription, revenue, and margin metrics in Revenue Cloud. Billing dashboards are grouped under Billing Central and display billing and revenue data, including invoice health, receivables, account balances, and recurring revenue. Revenue Cloud Operations Console separately supports operational troubleshooting through pricing execution logs and product configuration logs.",
  components: [
    {id:"revenue-dashboards",name:"Revenue Dashboards",icon:"\u{1F4CA}",desc:"This graph component corresponds to the documented Revenue Management Intelligence Dashboards capability in Revenue Cloud. These Tableau Einstein dashboards use data integrated with Data 360 to visualize key performance indicators across billing, pricing, order fulfillment, subscriptions, and revenue lifecycle activity. Administrators must complete Set Up Revenue Management Intelligence and Install Revenue Management Intelligence Apps before users access the dashboards.",tags:["RevenueDashboard","DashboardComponent","DashboardFilter"],docs:["Revenue Management Intelligence offers a suite of Tableau Einstein dashboards for evaluating revenue strategies and operational performance in Revenue Cloud. Data 360 provides the data foundation for detailed visualizations and analysis of key performance indicators. The dashboards consolidate metrics into visual insights for sales, finance, and operations teams.","Revenue Management Intelligence dashboards include Billing Analytics, Pricing Analytics, Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator Analytics, and Subscription and Revenue Lifecycle Analytics. Pricing Analytics covers revenue, cost, margin, customer, product, Price Volume Mix, Price Waterfall, and Price Volume Trend analysis. Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator Analytics provides Order Analysis and Order Details dashboards, while Subscription and Revenue Lifecycle Analytics covers subscription performance, renewals, and churn.","Billing Analytics dashboards are grouped under Billing Central and display billing and revenue data. The Overview dashboard covers revenue recognition, recurring revenue, net receivables, account activity, and tax generation failures. The Invoices dashboard covers invoice health, failures, disputes, credit memos, aging, and invoice details, while the embedded Account Insights dashboard shows account balances, outstanding invoices, credits, payments, and invoice aging. Billing Analytics requires the Revenue Cloud Advanced license and the Revenue Cloud Billing license."],connections:[{"planet":"billing","desc":"Dashboards display billing and revenue data"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Dashboards show pipeline and deal analytics"}]},
    {id:"revenue-insights",name:"Revenue Insights",icon:"\u{1F4A1}",desc:"The documentation doesn’t define a separately named feature called Revenue Insights. This graph component represents the analysis and actionable metrics delivered through Revenue Management Intelligence and its dashboards in Revenue Cloud. Those dashboards support analysis of subscription, pricing, order fulfillment, billing, customer, product, revenue, margin, renewal, and churn data.",tags:["RevenueInsight","InsightRecommendation","TrendAnalysis"],docs:["Revenue Management Intelligence dashboards consolidate key performance metrics into actionable visual insights rather than delivering a separate Revenue Insights feature. Users can examine revenue and margin performance, pricing changes, customer and product contribution, fulfillment progress, invoice health, subscription growth, renewals, and churn. Dashboard filters support focused analysis for relevant accounts, products, regions, industries, and time periods where documented.","The analysis is delivered through Revenue Management Intelligence apps installed after Revenue Cloud data is ingested into Data 360. Administrators deploy the appropriate data bundles and install the apps for Pricing Analytics, Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator Analytics, and Billing Analytics. Users then access the resulting dashboards from locations such as Billing Central, the Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator home page, Pricing Pulse, and the Tableau Next app."],connections:[{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Pricing insights identify discount trends and margin opportunities"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Billing insights highlight collection patterns and revenue leakage"}]},
    {id:"operations-console",name:"Operations Console",icon:"\u{1F4BB}",desc:"Revenue Cloud Operations Console is documented as a separate top-level capability from Revenue Management Intelligence. It provides operational visibility into pricing executions and product configuration behavior in Revenue Cloud. Users can inspect pricing API execution logs and configuration logs to troubleshoot errors, analyze outcomes, and review performance.",tags:["OperationsConsole","SystemHealth","ErrorQueue","AlertConfiguration"],docs:["Revenue Cloud Operations Console provides a centralized location for monitoring and troubleshooting pricing procedures and configurations. Its pricing views show Price Waterfall storage, decision-table usage, API-call usage, and Pricing API Executions. Pricing execution records include details such as execution ID, creation date, status, API endpoint, reference key, and the user who executed the API.","All headless pricing and pricing API executions are recorded as pricing logs, including successful and unsuccessful executions and the discovery and pricing procedures performed within an API call. The Details and Debug Details tabs show execution status, procedure information, line-item results, involved elements, error reasons, and troubleshooting guidance. Advanced Price Logs can capture input values and exception details for selected complex pricing elements, but the documentation recommends enabling them only during active troubleshooting.","Configuration Logs capture product configuration behavior for analysis in Revenue Cloud Operations Console. After the required permission and Decision Explainer setup, users can enable logging from Product Configurator, Product Catalog Management, or the Configure with Logging action in Transaction Line Editor. The Configuration Logs tab then shows engine actions, action statistics, reactions, reaction details, and reaction statistics for the selected configuration."],connections:[{"planet":"procedures","desc":"Console shows batch processing status and results"},{"planet":"orchestrator","desc":"Console displays fulfillment progress and blockers"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Set up Revenue Management Intelligence and its Data 360 connection.","Deploy the required Revenue Management Intelligence data bundles and refresh their data streams.","Install the applicable Revenue Management Intelligence apps.","Review billing and revenue data in the Billing Central dashboards.","Analyze pricing, fulfillment, subscription, renewal, churn, revenue, and margin metrics in the relevant dashboards.","Use Revenue Cloud Operations Console separately to inspect pricing execution logs and configuration logs."],
  connections: [{"planet":"billing","desc":"Dashboards display billing and revenue data"}]
},

setup: {
  packages: ["core"],
  name: "Setup and Administration",
  icon: "\u{2699}",
  color: "#d946ef",
  description: "Setup and Administration is the control center for planning, enabling, securing, and maintaining a Revenue Cloud implementation. It coordinates implementation readiness, user permissions, Revenue Settings, catalog access, and billing configuration such as billing policies and tax engines. It also supports migration and coexistence with Salesforce CPQ and Salesforce Billing, release upgrades, and customer- or partner-facing experiences built with Experience Cloud.",
  components: [
    {id:"implementation-planning",name:"Implementation Planning",icon:"\u{1F5FA}",desc:"Implementation Planning prepares an organization to configure and deploy Revenue Cloud within its broader Salesforce platform strategy. Planning includes assembling qualified internal experts and partner implementers, reviewing limits and considerations, and completing preconfiguration steps. Teams should account for required administrative, development, integration, deployment, and Salesforce product skills before beginning configuration.",tags:["ImplementationPlan","ReadinessCheck","SetupChecklist","FeatureDependency"],docs:["Build an implementation team that combines business expertise, Salesforce administration, development, and implementation experience. Administrators should understand the Salesforce platform and relevant capabilities such as Sales Cloud, Experience Cloud, Business Rules Engine, Context Service, Decision Table, Decision Explainer, and Omnistudio. Salesforce recommends that at least one team member develop Salesforce administration skills and pursue the Salesforce Certified Administrator certification.","Include developers who can translate business requirements into Revenue Cloud customizations, extensions, and integrations. Relevant skills include REST APIs, Apex, Lightning Web Components, Salesforce Flows, source control, Salesforce CLI, and deployment tools. If the required expertise isn't available internally, a certified Salesforce implementation partner can assist with planning, customization, training, and ongoing support.","Review Revenue Cloud limits and considerations before designing or operating the quote-to-cash lifecycle. Determine the deployment model in the context of the organization's overall Salesforce org strategy, including any consolidation or multi-org plans involving Sales Cloud, Service Cloud, Experience Cloud, or Field Service. Involve the Salesforce account executive and platform architect early because the selected org strategy affects more than the Revenue Cloud implementation itself."],connections:[]},
    {id:"permissions-users",name:"Permissions and Users",icon:"\u{1F512}",desc:"Permissions and Users controls access to Revenue Cloud features, objects, APIs, and administrative tasks. Setup guidance includes creating profiles and users and assigning permission sets appropriate to each persona. Some permission sets become available only after their associated features are enabled in Revenue Settings, so feature enablement and access assignment must be coordinated.",tags:["PermissionSet","PermissionSetGroup","UserAssignment","FieldLevelSecurity"],docs:["Assign permission sets according to the tasks each user performs. Revenue Cloud provides role-specific access for areas such as billing administration, billing operations, accounts receivable, payments, tax, fulfillment, usage, product configuration, and transaction management. Administrators must also grant access to Context Service objects and features when users need data exposed through context definition nodes and attributes.","Feature availability can determine whether a permission set appears in Setup. For example, Product Configuration Rules Designer and Product Configurator are available only after Configure Products at Runtime is enabled in Revenue Settings. Transaction Management setup also requires the appropriate permissions before administrators enable quote and order capture or related features.","Manage catalog access with the applicable Product Catalog Management permission sets for internal users and customers or partners with Experience Cloud accounts. Users of Guided Product Selection also require the OmniStudio User permission set in addition to Product Catalog Management access. The older Product Discovery permission set license and permission sets are deprecated, so organizations should assign Product Catalog Management permissions and unassign the deprecated Product Discovery permissions."],connections:[{"planet":"approvals","desc":"Permission levels determine approval authority"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Permissions control who can create and modify quotes and orders"}]},
    {id:"revenue-settings",name:"Revenue Settings",icon:"\u{1F527}",desc:"Revenue Settings is the central Setup page for enabling Revenue Cloud features and selecting shared runtime configuration. Administrators use it to activate capabilities such as Transaction Management, Product Configurator, configuration rule engines, pricing options, asset lifecycle features, and tax calculations for quotes and orders. It also coordinates catalog-related permissions and billing configuration, including billing policies and tax engines used by downstream billing processes.",tags:["RevenueSettings","BillingSettings","PricingSettings","SystemConfiguration"],docs:["Begin by turning on Enable Revenue Cloud Features when users require access to Revenue Cloud objects and Transaction Management capabilities. Additional settings enable application usage tagging, runtime product configuration, contract pricing, quote and order groups, ramp deals, transaction processing, Document Builder, Instant Pricing, and other supported sales experiences. Because feature dependencies and permissions affect enablement, administrators should verify required permission sets if a setting produces an error.","Use Revenue Settings to select procedures and context-dependent configuration used during transactions. The pricing procedure selected there determines pricing for quotes and orders, while separate selections in Product Discovery Settings and Salesforce Pricing Setup serve product discovery and headless calls. Revenue Settings also supports selecting a default rating discovery procedure, a default transaction processing type, and an asset context definition when Constraint Rules Engine models use asset data.","Configure billing and tax prerequisites before enabling estimated tax calculations for quotes and orders. Tax Configurations in Transaction Management requires a Tax Engine Provider and Tax Engine along with tax policies and treatments. After Add Estimated Taxes to Quotes and Orders is enabled, verify that tax attributes are mapped and synchronized in SalesTransactionContext or its extended context definition so calculations use accurate data.","Salesforce Go provides a more automated path for discovering and setting up Salesforce features. It brings permission assignment, prerequisite configuration, feature setup, and progress monitoring together in one place. The broader Revenue Cloud setup sequence also links administrators to setup for Product Catalog Management, Salesforce Pricing, Rate Management, Product Configurator, Dynamic Revenue Orchestrator, Usage Management, and Billing."],connections:[{"planet":"billing","desc":"Billing policies and tax engines are configured here"},{"planet":"pricing","desc":"Pricing defaults and rounding rules are set here"}]},
    {id:"migration-upgrades",name:"Migration and Upgrades",icon:"\u{1F4E4}",desc:"Migration and Upgrades governs the transition from Salesforce CPQ and Salesforce Billing to Revenue Cloud and the maintenance of an implementation across releases. Migration planning covers strategy selection, transactional data scope, data dependencies, tools, cutover, reporting, change management, and common issues. Upgrade guidance and release-specific known issues help administrators prepare an existing Revenue Cloud implementation for a newer release.",tags:["MigrationTool","DataMigration","LegacyMapping","CoexistenceConfig"],docs:["Evaluate the migration strategy in conjunction with operational planning and the organization's Salesforce org strategy. A transition can involve a period when Salesforce CPQ and Revenue Cloud coexist, whether Revenue Cloud is deployed in the same org or a new org. Teams should plan this coexistence deliberately and align the deployment decision with any broader single-org or multi-org strategy.","Retain at least one active Salesforce CPQ license throughout the migration period in production and in every sandbox where Revenue Cloud is configured or tested. The license preserves access to Salesforce CPQ features, historical data, configuration, and reports for reference, validation, or rollback. CPQ-installed sandboxes are required for coexistence validation, and integration-test orgs that reference CPQ objects also require an active CPQ package.","Define the transactional migration scope and account for dependencies before moving data. For asset migration, the recommended method is to create, backdate, and activate historical initial-sale orders so standard Revenue Cloud flows generate the assets; direct asset creation is an alternative when recreating transactional history isn't possible. Users who manage asset data must first have Revenue Cloud enabled and receive the Access Lifecycle-Managed Assets user permission.","Plan rollout and cutover together with post-migration reporting and organizational change management. Review the applicable Revenue Cloud upgrade guidance and release-specific known issues before applying release changes. Validation should include migrated data, coexistence scenarios, integrations, reports, and the configurations needed by active business processes."],connections:[]},
    {id:"experience-cloud",name:"Experience Cloud",icon:"\u{1F310}",desc:"Experience Cloud extends supported Revenue Cloud experiences to customer and partner site users. Setup addresses permission set licenses, supported site templates, supported Revenue Cloud tasks, and implementation requirements for Product Configurator and Transaction Management. Access depends on the user's Revenue Cloud license, and users with the Revenue Cloud Growth license don't have access to Experience Cloud sites.",tags:["ExperienceCloud","PortalComponent","SelfServiceConfig","CustomerPortal"],docs:["Assign Experience Cloud users the permission set licenses and permission sets required for the Revenue Cloud tasks they perform. For example, Billing Experience Cloud User lets billing site users view and download invoices, select a payment method, add a saved payment method, and pay outstanding invoice balances. Product Discovery access for customer and partner accounts uses the applicable Product Catalog Management permission set.","Build supported product-browsing experiences with Product Discovery Lightning components, including Product List, Product Details, Product Bundle Details, and Product Attribute Details. Experience Cloud sites that use these components must be created with the Build Your Own (Aura), Customer Service, or Customer Account Service template. Administrators add the components in Experience Builder, configure the record ID and component properties, and save the page.","Revenue Cloud documentation identifies implementation requirements for Product Configurator on Experience Cloud and the Transaction Management tasks supported on LWR sites. It also supports creating contracts from quotes and orders for partner users when the required access and configuration are in place. Community and partner access for Agentforce requires its own Revenue Cloud setup and considerations."],connections:[{"planet":"billing","desc":"Customers view and pay invoices through the portal"},{"planet":"transactions","desc":"Partners can create and manage orders through the portal"}]}
  ],
  dataFlow: ["Assemble the implementation team and review Revenue Cloud limits, dependencies, and deployment options.","Enable required Revenue Cloud features and configure shared procedures, contexts, billing policies, and tax engines.","Create users and assign role-appropriate permissions, including catalog and Experience Cloud access.","Configure and validate Revenue Cloud features in sandboxes before production rollout.","Migrate dependent data, test coexistence and integrations, execute cutover, and review upgrade guidance."],
  connections: [{"planet":"catalog","desc":"Catalog permissions managed in settings"},{"planet":"billing","desc":"Billing policies and tax engines in settings"}]
}

};
